Year 3,7-8,Spring,,Multiplication and division B,2.0,2,291,Related calculations,Related calculations,"This small step builds on the previous step and children’s existing knowledge of times-tables to explore calculations related to known facts. Children explore scaling facts by 10, for example using 3 × 4 = 12 to derive 3 × 40 = 120 and 30 × 4 = 120. A range of representations are used to expose the link between multiples of 1 and multiples of 10. Children begin by using base ten, before moving on to the slightly more abstract representation of place value counters. Children go on to explore this relationship with division, for example using 12 ÷ 3 = 4 to derive 120 ÷ 3 = 40. This will be revisited later in the block. Care should be taken to ensure that children do not also think that 12 ÷ 30 = 40. This is a good opportunity to remind them that multiplication is commutative while division is not.","This small step builds on the previous step and children’s existing knowledge of times-tables to explore calculations related to known facts. Children explore scaling facts by 10, for example using 3 × 4 = 12 to derive 3 × 40 = 120 and 30 × 4 = 120. A range of representations are used to expose the link between multiples of 1 and multiples of 10. Children begin by using base ten, before moving on to the slightly more abstract representation of place value counters. Children go on to explore this relationship with division, for example using 12 ÷ 3 = 4 to derive 120 ÷ 3 = 40. This will be revisited later in the block. Care should be taken to ensure that children do not also think that 12 ÷ 30 = 40. This is a good opportunity to remind them that multiplication is commutative while division is not.","This small step builds on the previous step and children’s existing knowledge of times-tables to explore calculations related to known facts. Children explore scaling facts by 10, for example using 3 × 4 = 12 to derive 3 × 40 = 120 and 30 × 4 = 120. A range of representations are