Year 3,7-8,Spring,,Length and perimeter,9.0,9,309,Subtract lengths,Subtract lengths,"begin by subtracting lengths that are measured in the same unit of measurement. They then look at subtracting milllimetres from a whole number of centimetres as well as centimetres from a whole number of metres using simple conversions, for example 1 m − 35 cm and 4 cm − 3 mm. They then explore more complex examples where the lengths have different units of measurement and therefore equivalent lengths need to be found, for example 4 m 36 cm − 112 cm. This can be a useful opportunity to also revisit subtraction where there is a need for exchange, for example 2 m 43 cm − 118 cm. Children should be exposed to the different structures of subtraction through word problems: partitioning, reduction and difference. Bar models can be a useful pictorial representation to highlight these different structures. Subtract lengths Year 3 | Spring term | Block 2 – Length and perimeter | Step 9","begin by subtracting lengths that are measured in the same unit of measurement. They then look at subtracting milllimetres from a whole number of centimetres as well as centimetres from a whole number of metres using simple conversions, for example 1 m − 35 cm and 4 cm − 3 mm. They then explore more complex examples where the lengths have different units of measurement and therefore equivalent lengths need to be found, for example 4 m 36 cm − 112 cm. This can be a useful opportunity to also revisit subtraction where there is a need for exchange, for example 2 m 43 cm − 118 cm. Children should be exposed to the different structures of subtraction through word problems: partitioning, reduction and difference. Bar models can be a useful pictorial representation to highlight these different structures. Subtract lengths Year 3 | Spring term | Block 2 – Length and perimeter | Step 9","begin by subtracting lengths that are measured in the same unit of measurement. They then look at subtracting milllimetres from a w