Year 3,7-8,Spring,,Mass and capacity,5.0,5,327,Compare mass,Compare mass,"compare the masses of different objects using grams and kilograms. In Year 2, children decided if an object was heavier or lighter by using balance scales. They now use units of measure to work out which object is heavier or lighter. Understanding that kilograms are heavier than grams will help them to compare mass, for example 100 g is lighter than 100 kg. They can also compare using fractions: for example 1 2 kg is heavier than 400 g. Children then go on to compare masses that combine kilograms and grams. They should recognise that, because kilograms are heavier than grams, they should compare the kilograms first: for example 1 kg and 300 g is lighter than 3 kg and 300 g. If the kilograms are the same, they then need to compare the grams: for example 1 kg and 300 g is heavier than 1 kg and 100 g.","compare the masses of different objects using grams and kilograms. In Year 2, children decided if an object was heavier or lighter by using balance scales. They now use units of measure to work out which object is heavier or lighter. Understanding that kilograms are heavier than grams will help them to compare mass, for example 100 g is lighter than 100 kg. They can also compare using fractions: for example 1 2 kg is heavier than 400 g. Children then go on to compare masses that combine kilograms and grams. They should recognise that, because kilograms are heavier than grams, they should compare the kilograms first: for example 1 kg and 300 g is lighter than 3 kg and 300 g. If the kilograms are the same, they then need to compare the grams: for example 1 kg and 300 g is heavier than 1 kg and 100 g.","compare the masses of different objects using grams and kilograms. In Year 2, children decided if an object was heavier or lighter by using balance scales. They now use units of measure to work out which object is heavier or lighter. Understanding that kilograms are heavier than grams will help them t