Year 4,8-9,Summer,,Shape,7.0,7,486,Lines of symmetry,Lines of symmetry,"Children first found vertical lines of symmetry within a shape in Year 2. In Year 3, this was extended to horizontal and vertical lines of symmetry. In this small step, that learning is extended further to include any line of symmetry in any direction. Begin by recapping what a line of symmetry is. The use of mirrors is helpful to reinforce this understanding, as is cutting out shapes and folding them. Another useful activity is putting two congruent shapes together to form symmetrical shapes. Children look for lines of symmetry in any orientation within any 2-D shape. They then sort shapes by the number of lines of symmetry. They can also explore regular polygons, discovering that the number of lines of symmetry in a regular polygon is the same as the number of sides.","Children first found vertical lines of symmetry within a shape in Year 2. In Year 3, this was extended to horizontal and vertical lines of symmetry. In this small step, that learning is extended further to include any line of symmetry in any direction. Begin by recapping what a line of symmetry is. The use of mirrors is helpful to reinforce this understanding, as is cutting out shapes and folding them. Another useful activity is putting two congruent shapes together to form symmetrical shapes. Children look for lines of symmetry in any orientation within any 2-D shape. They then sort shapes by the number of lines of symmetry. They can also explore regular polygons, discovering that the number of lines of symmetry in a regular polygon is the same as the number of sides.","Children first found vertical lines of symmetry within a shape in Year 2. In Year 3, this was extended to horizontal and vertical lines of symmetry. In this small step, that learning is extended further to include any line of symmetry in any direction. Begin by recapping what a line of symmetry is. The use of mirrors is helpful to reinforce this understanding, a