Year 6,10-11,Summer,,Shape,1.0,1,736,Measure and classify angles,Measure and classify angles,"In Year 4, children encountered the classifications of angles as acute, right and obtuse. In Year 5, this learning was extended to include the use of degrees, as well as reflex angles. This small step revisits that learning by classifying angles and measuring them with a protractor. Begin by recapping the types of angles. Move on to using a protractor to measure an angle, taking care when modelling which scale to use. Encourage children to estimate the size of an angle before measuring it, as they are then less likely to read the wrong scale on the protractor. For example, if an angle is seen to be less than a right angle, its size will be less than 90°. Children should practise estimating angles by comparing them to known fractions of a turn. Similarly, classifying angles first can support children in reading the correct scale. For example, if an angle is acute, then the size of the angle in degrees must be less than 90","In Year 4, children encountered the classifications of angles as acute, right and obtuse. In Year 5, this learning was extended to include the use of degrees, as well as reflex angles. This small step revisits that learning by classifying angles and measuring them with a protractor. Begin by recapping the types of angles. Move on to using a protractor to measure an angle, taking care when modelling which scale to use. Encourage children to estimate the size of an angle before measuring it, as they are then less likely to read the wrong scale on the protractor. For example, if an angle is seen to be less than a right angle, its size will be less than 90°. Children should practise estimating angles by comparing them to known fractions of a turn. Similarly, classifying angles first can support children in reading the correct scale. For example, if an angle is acute, then the size of the angle in degrees must be less than 90","In Year 4, children encountered 