Year 7,11-12,Autumn,,Rounding and estimation,4.0,4,805,Solve problems with estimation,Solve problems with estimation,"In this small step, students build on their understanding from the previous step to use estimation to solve problems. Remind students that rounding given values to 1 significant figure is a useful strategy when estimating with calculations. Present problems in a variety of contexts, for example the area of a shape or a total cost. Encourage students to discuss whether their estimates are over- or underestimates and to reflect on whether their answers are realistic and/or within a reasonable range. This approach enhances estimation skills, making estimation meaningful in various contexts. Calculations that involve subtractions or divisions may require greater attention when considering an over- or underestimate due to some cases behaving counterintuitively. For example, 39 ÷ 1.57 ≈ 40 ÷ 2 = 20, which is an underestimate despite both values being rounded up. If appropriate, students could use calculators to check their answers.","In this small step, students build on their understanding from the previous step to use estimation to solve problems. Remind students that rounding given values to 1 significant figure is a useful strategy when estimating with calculations. Present problems in a variety of contexts, for example the area of a shape or a total cost. Encourage students to discuss whether their estimates are over- or underestimates and to reflect on whether their answers are realistic and/or within a reasonable range. This approach enhances estimation skills, making estimation meaningful in various contexts. Calculations that involve subtractions or divisions may require greater attention when considering an over- or underestimate due to some cases behaving counterintuitively. For example, 39 ÷ 1.57 ≈ 40 ÷ 2 = 20, which is an underestimate despite both values being rounded up. If appropriate, students could use calculators to check their answers."