Year 7,11-12,Autumn,,Rounding and estimation,5.0,5,806,Understand and use error interval notation E,Understand and use error interval notation E,"In this extend step, students build on the inequality notation covered earlier in the year to formally represent the upper and lower bounds of a number that has been rounded to a given degree of accuracy. Recap inequality notation to ensure that students are comfortable using the <, >, ≤ and ≥ symbols. The use of a number line can support students to understand the range that the true value lies within. Build students’ familiarity with error intervals by starting with values rounded to powers of 10, before exploring decimals. Discuss the convention that the true value can be equal to the lower bound but not the upper bound. It may also be appropriate to discuss the fact that, for example, 3.499999999… = 3.5, so stating that an upper bound is a terminating decimal is accepted.","In this extend step, students build on the inequality notation covered earlier in the year to formally represent the upper and lower bounds of a number that has been rounded to a given degree of accuracy. Recap inequality notation to ensure that students are comfortable using the <, >, ≤ and ≥ symbols. The use of a number line can support students to understand the range that the true value lies within. Build students’ familiarity with error intervals by starting with values rounded to powers of 10, before exploring decimals. Discuss the convention that the true value can be equal to the lower bound but not the upper bound. It may also be appropriate to discuss the fact that, for example, 3.499999999… = 3.5, so stating that an upper bound is a terminating decimal is accepted.","In this extend step, students build on the inequality notation covered earlier in the year to formally represent the upper and lower bounds of a number that has been rounded to a given degree of accuracy. Recap inequality notation to ensure that students are comfortable using