Year 7,11-12,Spring,,Graphing data,8.0,8,814,Lines of best fit,Lines of best fit,"In this small step, students draw and interpret lines of best fit on a scatter graph. In Maths, a line of best fit is a straight line that best represents the overall trend between two variables and is used to predict future or missing values. A positive correlation produces a straight upward-sloping line, and a negative correlation a downward slope. If there is no clear correlation, a line of best fit cannot be drawn. Discuss how closely data points cluster around the line to indicate the strength of the relationship. Draw attention to data points that do not follow the general trend. Explain that these are called outliers and discuss possible reasons for these values. It is worth noting that outliers in a scientific context are referred to as anomalous results or an anomaly.","In this small step, students draw and interpret lines of best fit on a scatter graph. In Maths, a line of best fit is a straight line that best represents the overall trend between two variables and is used to predict future or missing values. A positive correlation produces a straight upward-sloping line, and a negative correlation a downward slope. If there is no clear correlation, a line of best fit cannot be drawn. Discuss how closely data points cluster around the line to indicate the strength of the relationship. Draw attention to data points that do not follow the general trend. Explain that these are called outliers and discuss possible reasons for these values. It is worth noting that outliers in a scientific context are referred to as anomalous results or an anomaly.","In this small step, students draw and interpret lines of best fit on a scatter graph. In Maths, a line of best fit is a straight line that best represents the overall trend between two variables and is used to predict future or missing values. A positive correlation produces a straight upward-sloping line, and a negative correlation a d