Year 7,11-12,Spring,,Fractions decimals and percentages,10.0,10,826,Compare and order fractions,Compare and order fractions,"In this small step, students compare and order fractions. Bar models are useful to support understanding. Start with fractions that have the same denominator and compare numerators, followed by fractions with different denominators, where one is a multiple of the other, and then unrelated denominators using common multiples. To compare fractions with the same numerator, students need to understand that a greater denominator means a smaller fraction. For each comparison, they should consider whether it is more efficient to use a common numerator or common denominator. Comparing fractions to numbers such as 0, 1 or 1 2 is another useful strategy, for example 7 10 > 3 8 because 7 10 > 1 2 and 3 8 < 1 2. This helps to develop number sense and supports more efficient comparisons.","In this small step, students compare and order fractions. Bar models are useful to support understanding. Start with fractions that have the same denominator and compare numerators, followed by fractions with different denominators, where one is a multiple of the other, and then unrelated denominators using common multiples. To compare fractions with the same numerator, students need to understand that a greater denominator means a smaller fraction. For each comparison, they should consider whether it is more efficient to use a common numerator or common denominator. Comparing fractions to numbers such as 0, 1 or 1 2 is another useful strategy, for example 7 10 > 3 8 because 7 10 > 1 2 and 3 8 < 1 2. This helps to develop number sense and supports more efficient comparisons.","In this small step, students compare and order fractions. Bar models are useful to support understanding. Start with fractions that have the same denominator and compare numerators, followed by fractions with different denominators, where one is a multiple of the other, and then unrelated denominat