Year 7,11-12,Spring,,Fractions and percentages of amounts,5.0,5,843,Percentage increase and decrease,Percentage increase and decrease,"In this small step, students increase or decrease an amount by a percentage, using both non-calculator and calculator methods. Students need to understand that they are subtracting/adding the given percentage from/to the original 100%. A bar model is a good way to visually represent the problem, helping students to determine whether they need to add or subtract. Encourage them to check that their answers make sense by considering if the answer should be greater or smaller than the original amount. Introducing the use of a decimal multiplier, such as 1.2 or 0.7, to increase and decrease values may extend students’ understanding, but it is not essential at this stage as it will be explicitly covered in Year 8","In this small step, students increase or decrease an amount by a percentage, using both non-calculator and calculator methods. Students need to understand that they are subtracting/adding the given percentage from/to the original 100%. A bar model is a good way to visually represent the problem, helping students to determine whether they need to add or subtract. Encourage them to check that their answers make sense by considering if the answer should be greater or smaller than the original amount. Introducing the use of a decimal multiplier, such as 1.2 or 0.7, to increase and decrease values may extend students’ understanding, but it is not essential at this stage as it will be explicitly covered in Year 8","In this small step, students increase or decrease an amount by a percentage, using both non-calculator and calculator methods. Students need to understand that they are subtracting/adding the given percentage from/to the original 100%. A bar model is a good way to visually represent the problem, helping students to determine whether they need to add or subtract. Encourage them to check that their answers make sense by consi