Year 7,11-12,Spring,,Fractions and percentages of amounts,6.0,6,844,Use a percentage to find the whole E,Use a percentage to find the whole E,"In this small step, students use a given percentage to calculate the whole. This builds on their previous understanding of percentages, particularly the concept that 100% represents the whole. Start with percentages that are easy to scale to 100%, such as 10%, 50% and 25%. Bar models are a useful representation, highlighting the percentage that the amount represents and helping students to relate this to finding the whole. As their confidence develops, students can find the whole from other percentages, such as 30% and 75%. Encourage them to determine if they can calculate 10% or 25% of the given percentage before scaling up to 100%. For percentages such as 47%, guide them to find the value of 1% and then to scale up to 100%. A calculator is recommended for these calculations.","In this small step, students use a given percentage to calculate the whole. This builds on their previous understanding of percentages, particularly the concept that 100% represents the whole. Start with percentages that are easy to scale to 100%, such as 10%, 50% and 25%. Bar models are a useful representation, highlighting the percentage that the amount represents and helping students to relate this to finding the whole. As their confidence develops, students can find the whole from other percentages, such as 30% and 75%. Encourage them to determine if they can calculate 10% or 25% of the given percentage before scaling up to 100%. For percentages such as 47%, guide them to find the value of 1% and then to scale up to 100%. A calculator is recommended for these calculations.","In this small step, students use a given percentage to calculate the whole. This builds on their previous understanding of percentages, particularly the concept that 100% represents the whole. Start with percentages that are easy to scale to 100%, such as 10%, 50% and 25%. Bar