Year 7,11-12,Summer,,Speed distance and time,8.0,8,871,Interpret distance-time graphs,Interpret distance-time graphs,"In this small step, students interpret journeys represented on distance–time graphs. Explain that the horizontal axis of a distance–time graph represents time and the vertical axis represents distance. Highlight the key features of distance–time graphs, such as diagonal line segments indicating motion and horizontal line segments representing when an object is stationary. Students should also understand that when a graph begins and ends at the horizontal axis, the journey represented is a return journey. Ensure that distance–time graphs are presented with different scales, for example altering the number of intervals used on the time axis so that 1 unit represents 20 minutes rather than 30 minutes.","In this small step, students interpret journeys represented on distance–time graphs. Explain that the horizontal axis of a distance–time graph represents time and the vertical axis represents distance. Highlight the key features of distance–time graphs, such as diagonal line segments indicating motion and horizontal line segments representing when an object is stationary. Students should also understand that when a graph begins and ends at the horizontal axis, the journey represented is a return journey. Ensure that distance–time graphs are presented with different scales, for example altering the number of intervals used on the time axis so that 1 unit represents 20 minutes rather than 30 minutes.","In this small step, students interpret journeys represented on distance–time graphs. Explain that the horizontal axis of a distance–time graph represents time and the vertical axis represents distance. Highlight the key features of distance–time graphs, such as diagonal line segments indicating motion and horizontal line segments representing when an object is stationary. Students should also understand that when a graph begins and ends at the horizontal axi