Year 8,12-13,Autumn,,Algebraic manipulation,1.0,1,913,Form algebraic expressions,Form algebraic expressions,"In this small step, students form algebraic expressions, building on the notation and techniques met in Year 7 Students may need reminding that × and ÷ signs are not used in expressions, numbers precede letters and repeated multiplication of a variable is written using exponents (for example, aa is written as a2). Begin by exploring simple expressions such as “5 less than k”, explaining how this differs from “k less than 5”. As students’ confidence develops, move on to writing more complex expressions and apply these to a variety of contexts, such as the perimeter of shapes. To reinforce these concepts, students can use a variety of representations to support understanding, for example concrete resources, such as cubes and counters, and function machines.","In this small step, students form algebraic expressions, building on the notation and techniques met in Year 7 Students may need reminding that × and ÷ signs are not used in expressions, numbers precede letters and repeated multiplication of a variable is written using exponents (for example, aa is written as a2). Begin by exploring simple expressions such as “5 less than k”, explaining how this differs from “k less than 5”. As students’ confidence develops, move on to writing more complex expressions and apply these to a variety of contexts, such as the perimeter of shapes. To reinforce these concepts, students can use a variety of representations to support understanding, for example concrete resources, such as cubes and counters, and function machines.","In this small step, students form algebraic expressions, building on the notation and techniques met in Year 7 Students may need reminding that × and ÷ signs are not used in expressions, numbers precede letters and repeated multiplication of a variable is written using exponents (for example, aa is written as a2). Begin by exploring simple expressions s