Year 8,12-13,Autumn,,Algebraic manipulation,7.0,7,919,Factorise into a single bracket,Factorise into a single bracket,"In this small step, students factorise expressions into single brackets. Students do not always link factorising expressions with factors of numbers, so it is important to make this connection explicit, reinforcing the vocabulary ""common factor"" and ""highest common factor"". It can be useful to practise finding the highest common factor of a variety of terms, including those where a variable is a factor. Show students that factorising is the inverse of expanding a bracket. Begin with examples with positive numerical factors, before progressing to negative factors, algebraic factors and a combination of numerical and algebraic factors. Algebra tiles or area models are useful representations to support students and help them to make connections to their previous work on expanding brackets. Encourage students to check their answers by expanding.","In this small step, students factorise expressions into single brackets. Students do not always link factorising expressions with factors of numbers, so it is important to make this connection explicit, reinforcing the vocabulary ""common factor"" and ""highest common factor"". It can be useful to practise finding the highest common factor of a variety of terms, including those where a variable is a factor. Show students that factorising is the inverse of expanding a bracket. Begin with examples with positive numerical factors, before progressing to negative factors, algebraic factors and a combination of numerical and algebraic factors. Algebra tiles or area models are useful representations to support students and help them to make connections to their previous work on expanding brackets. Encourage students to check their answers by expanding.","In this small step, students factorise expressions into single brackets. Students do not always link factorising expressions with factors of numbers, so it is im