Year 8,12-13,Autumn,,Multiply and divide fractions,6.0,6,941,Divide a fraction by a unit fraction,Divide a fraction by a unit fraction,"In this small step, students divide a fraction by a unit fraction. Start by dividing fractions with common denominators. Bar models can be used to support students’ understanding. Using language such as, “How many quarters are there in three-quarters?” can help students connect the pictorial model to the abstract concept of division. As their confidence develops, progress to dividing fractions with different denominators, introducing the reciprocal method. Remind students that using the reciprocal of the divisor allows them to transform the division problem into a multiplication problem, making it simpler to solve. An equivalent fraction wall can be a helpful visual aid to support students in creating equivalent fractions and recognising how the reciprocal method applies.","In this small step, students divide a fraction by a unit fraction. Start by dividing fractions with common denominators. Bar models can be used to support students’ understanding. Using language such as, “How many quarters are there in three-quarters?” can help students connect the pictorial model to the abstract concept of division. As their confidence develops, progress to dividing fractions with different denominators, introducing the reciprocal method. Remind students that using the reciprocal of the divisor allows them to transform the division problem into a multiplication problem, making it simpler to solve. An equivalent fraction wall can be a helpful visual aid to support students in creating equivalent fractions and recognising how the reciprocal method applies.","In this small step, students divide a fraction by a unit fraction. Start by dividing fractions with common denominators. Bar models can be used to support students’ understanding. Using language such as, “How many quarters are there in three-quarters?” can help students connect the pictorial m