Year 8,12-13,Autumn,,Symmetry and reflection,4.0,4,948,Reflect a shape in a diagonal line,Reflect a shape in a diagonal line,"In this small step, students develop their understanding of reflection and symmetry by reflecting shapes in a diagonal line that is at 45° between the horizontal and vertical. A useful strategy is to rotate the diagram by 45°, so that the mirror line is either vertical or horizontal. Students may then find it easier to count the perpendicular distance from the object’s vertices to the mirror line and plot a point the same distance on the other side of the mirror line. They could also use tracing paper to complete reflections. Begin by reflecting shapes on a grid, ensuring that the objects are presented on both sides of the mirror line. As confidence develops, reflect shapes on a coordinate grid in the lines y = x and y = −x.","In this small step, students develop their understanding of reflection and symmetry by reflecting shapes in a diagonal line that is at 45° between the horizontal and vertical. A useful strategy is to rotate the diagram by 45°, so that the mirror line is either vertical or horizontal. Students may then find it easier to count the perpendicular distance from the object’s vertices to the mirror line and plot a point the same distance on the other side of the mirror line. They could also use tracing paper to complete reflections. Begin by reflecting shapes on a grid, ensuring that the objects are presented on both sides of the mirror line. As confidence develops, reflect shapes on a coordinate grid in the lines y = x and y = −x.","In this small step, students develop their understanding of reflection and symmetry by reflecting shapes in a diagonal line that is at 45° between the horizontal and vertical. A useful strategy is to rotate the diagram by 45°, so that the mirror line is either vertical or horizontal. Students may then find it easier to count the perpendicular distance from the object’s vertices to the mirror line 