Year 8,12-13,Autumn,,Symmetry and reflection,6.0,6,950,Describe a reflection E,Describe a reflection E,"In this extend step, students use their understanding of reflection and symmetry to fully describe the single transformation from an object to an image after a reflection. Start with reflections of simple shapes such as rectangles and triangles. Discuss that the object and image are congruent and that each vertex from the object and image should be the same distance from the mirror line. Model finding the mirror line by counting the distance between the object and image and halving it. Using tracing paper or paper folding are also useful strategies. Once students are confident in identifying the position of the mirror line, discuss writing its equation. It may be helpful to recap the naming conventions of diagonal lines and equations of lines parallel to the axes.","In this extend step, students use their understanding of reflection and symmetry to fully describe the single transformation from an object to an image after a reflection. Start with reflections of simple shapes such as rectangles and triangles. Discuss that the object and image are congruent and that each vertex from the object and image should be the same distance from the mirror line. Model finding the mirror line by counting the distance between the object and image and halving it. Using tracing paper or paper folding are also useful strategies. Once students are confident in identifying the position of the mirror line, discuss writing its equation. It may be helpful to recap the naming conventions of diagonal lines and equations of lines parallel to the axes.","In this extend step, students use their understanding of reflection and symmetry to fully describe the single transformation from an object to an image after a reflection. Start with reflections of simple shapes such as rectangles and triangles. Discuss that the object and image are congruent and that each vertex from the object and image s