Year 8,12-13,Spring,,Area volume and density,5.0,5,955,Volume of cubes and cuboids,Volume of cubes and cuboids,"In this small step, students calculate the volumes of cubes and cuboids. Clarify that volume measures the space inside a 3-D object and is expressed in cubic units, while area measures the space in two dimensions. Use physical models such as interlocking cubes to help students visualise how volume fills a 3-D shape. Begin by counting layers of cubes before introducing the formula volume = length × width × height. Discuss how the three dimensions can be used interchangeably, and that the word “depth” may also be used. Model looking at cuboids from different orientations to identify the dimensions. Remind students that cubes are special cuboids, in which the dimensions are all equal, so the formula simplifies to the cube of the side length. If appropriate, challenge students to calculate the volumes of cuboids that require unit conversions.","In this small step, students calculate the volumes of cubes and cuboids. Clarify that volume measures the space inside a 3-D object and is expressed in cubic units, while area measures the space in two dimensions. Use physical models such as interlocking cubes to help students visualise how volume fills a 3-D shape. Begin by counting layers of cubes before introducing the formula volume = length × width × height. Discuss how the three dimensions can be used interchangeably, and that the word “depth” may also be used. Model looking at cuboids from different orientations to identify the dimensions. Remind students that cubes are special cuboids, in which the dimensions are all equal, so the formula simplifies to the cube of the side length. If appropriate, challenge students to calculate the volumes of cuboids that require unit conversions.","In this small step, students calculate the volumes of cubes and cuboids. Clarify that volume measures the space inside a 3-D object and is expressed in cubic units, while area measur