Year 8,12-13,Spring,,Area volume and density,7.0,7,957,"Understand the units of density, mass and volume","Understand the units of density, mass and volume","In this small step, students explore density, mass and volume, developing a deeper understanding of the units involved. Introduce density through familiar comparisons, such as looking at how the same mass of different materials occupies different amounts of space. For example, ask if a tonne of bricks takes up the same volume as a tonne of feathers. For objects of similar size, discuss why their masses differ, reinforcing the link between mass, volume and density. Emphasise the importance of using the correct units and introduce the concept of “per” (mass per unit volume) by relating it to familiar concepts such as mph or km/h. Examine the density of various materials, including solids, liquids and gases, across different volumes and encourage discussion on selecting appropriate units for different objects.","In this small step, students explore density, mass and volume, developing a deeper understanding of the units involved. Introduce density through familiar comparisons, such as looking at how the same mass of different materials occupies different amounts of space. For example, ask if a tonne of bricks takes up the same volume as a tonne of feathers. For objects of similar size, discuss why their masses differ, reinforcing the link between mass, volume and density. Emphasise the importance of using the correct units and introduce the concept of “per” (mass per unit volume) by relating it to familiar concepts such as mph or km/h. Examine the density of various materials, including solids, liquids and gases, across different volumes and encourage discussion on selecting appropriate units for different objects.","In this small step, students explore density, mass and volume, developing a deeper understanding of the units involved. Introduce density through familiar comparisons, such as looking at how the same 