Year 8,12-13,Spring,,Area volume and density,8.0,8,958,"Solve problems with density, mass and volume","Solve problems with density, mass and volume","In this small step, students build on the previous step to solve problems with density, mass and volume. Reinforce that density is the mass per unit of volume and introduce the formula, density = mass volume . It can be helpful to model the calculation of density using a range of common materials, for example wood, metal and plastic, asking students to consider if they think the material will be more or less dense than the previous one. Once students are confident with calculating the density of an object, they can use the formula to calculate the volume or mass of an object. Calculators can be used for support. Encourage students to check that their answers and units make sense for the given context.","In this small step, students build on the previous step to solve problems with density, mass and volume. Reinforce that density is the mass per unit of volume and introduce the formula, density = mass volume . It can be helpful to model the calculation of density using a range of common materials, for example wood, metal and plastic, asking students to consider if they think the material will be more or less dense than the previous one. Once students are confident with calculating the density of an object, they can use the formula to calculate the volume or mass of an object. Calculators can be used for support. Encourage students to check that their answers and units make sense for the given context.","In this small step, students build on the previous step to solve problems with density, mass and volume. Reinforce that density is the mass per unit of volume and introduce the formula, density = mass volume . It can be helpful to model the calculation of density using a range of common materials, for example wood, metal and plastic, asking students to consider if they think the material will be more or less dense than t