Year 8,12-13,Spring,,Indices,2.0,2,982,Multiply and divide expressions with indices,Multiply and divide expressions with indices,"In this small step, students multiply and divide expressions with indices, building on their understanding of simplifying expressions from earlier in the year. Remind students of the conventions for expressions such as 3 × a, b × 4 and c × c, as well as the use of fractions to represent division. Students explore powers, for example recognising that x5 is equivalent to x × x × x × x × x. Encourage them to write calculations out in full to help develop understanding, for example 33 32 = 3 × 3 × 3 3 × 3 . This will help to prepare them for higher powers and the laws of indices later in the block. Also remind students to express answers in fractional form, such as y 2 , rather than using decimals (0.5y).","In this small step, students multiply and divide expressions with indices, building on their understanding of simplifying expressions from earlier in the year. Remind students of the conventions for expressions such as 3 × a, b × 4 and c × c, as well as the use of fractions to represent division. Students explore powers, for example recognising that x5 is equivalent to x × x × x × x × x. Encourage them to write calculations out in full to help develop understanding, for example 33 32 = 3 × 3 × 3 3 × 3 . This will help to prepare them for higher powers and the laws of indices later in the block. Also remind students to express answers in fractional form, such as y 2 , rather than using decimals (0.5y).","In this small step, students multiply and divide expressions with indices, building on their understanding of simplifying expressions from earlier in the year. Remind students of the conventions for expressions such as 3 × a, b × 4 and c × c, as well as the use of fractions to represent division. Students explore powers, for example recognising that x5 is equivalent to x × x × x × x × x. Encourage them to write calculations out in full to h