Year 9,13-14,Autumn,,Equations inequalities and formulae,7.0,7,1076,Change the subject of a formula (one step),Change the subject of a formula (one step),"In this small step, students rearrange simple formulae that involve one step of working to change the subject. Drawing comparisons to solving equations is useful as students begin to explore this concept. Bar models could also be used to build ideas related to changing the subject of a formula, for example if a + b = c, then c − a = b and so on. Ensure that students understand that the subject of a formula is the variable that is isolated and equal to an expression involving other variables. Discuss the fact that the subject of a formula can appear on either the left- or right-hand side of the = sign; for example, r is the subject of both r = m + p and m + p = r. When working with formulae involving squares, highlight that square rooting will give two possible values; for example, if t2 = y, then t = ± y.","In this small step, students rearrange simple formulae that involve one step of working to change the subject. Drawing comparisons to solving equations is useful as students begin to explore this concept. Bar models could also be used to build ideas related to changing the subject of a formula, for example if a + b = c, then c − a = b and so on. Ensure that students understand that the subject of a formula is the variable that is isolated and equal to an expression involving other variables. Discuss the fact that the subject of a formula can appear on either the left- or right-hand side of the = sign; for example, r is the subject of both r = m + p and m + p = r. When working with formulae involving squares, highlight that square rooting will give two possible values; for example, if t2 = y, then t = ± y.","In this small step, students rearrange simple formulae that involve one step of working to change the subject. Drawing comparisons to solving equations is useful as students begin to explore this concept. Ba