Year 9,13-14,Autumn,,Equations inequalities and formulae,9.0,9,1078,Change the subject of a complex formula E,Change the subject of a complex formula E,"In this extend step, students rearrange more complex formulae that require multiple steps to rearrange. Begin by introducing formulae where grouped terms can be added or subtracted using one step, for example making p the subject of m = p + 2f by subtracting 2f from both sides. Students can then move on to strategies for rearranging formulae where the proposed subject has a negative coefficient, for example making t the subject of m = p − 2t. Students should also be given opportunities to rearrange formulae with squared/cubed terms and/or square/cube roots, discussing the fact that square rooting gives both a positive and a negative root. Familiar formulae from Maths and/or Science could be used throughout.","In this extend step, students rearrange more complex formulae that require multiple steps to rearrange. Begin by introducing formulae where grouped terms can be added or subtracted using one step, for example making p the subject of m = p + 2f by subtracting 2f from both sides. Students can then move on to strategies for rearranging formulae where the proposed subject has a negative coefficient, for example making t the subject of m = p − 2t. Students should also be given opportunities to rearrange formulae with squared/cubed terms and/or square/cube roots, discussing the fact that square rooting gives both a positive and a negative root. Familiar formulae from Maths and/or Science could be used throughout.","In this extend step, students rearrange more complex formulae that require multiple steps to rearrange. Begin by introducing formulae where grouped terms can be added or subtracted using one step, for example making p the subject of m = p + 2f by subtracting 2f from both sides. Students can then move on to strategies for rearranging formulae where the proposed subject has a negative coefficient, for exampl