Year 9,13-14,Autumn,,Rates,3.0,3,1084,Flow problems and their graphs,Flow problems and their graphs,"In this small step, students explore flow problems by comparing how different-shaped containers are filled or drained. Start by comparing the filling of containers in the shape of prisms or cylinders with identical heights but differing bases, highlighting the effect that the shape has on how quickly it fills. Show that the graphs related to the time taken and depth of liquid in each container form a straight line, as the rate of flow is constant. Move on to containers with non-parallel sides, for example a conical or boiling flask, to draw out that the depth of the water will increase more quickly when the container is narrower and more slowly when it is wider. Show that, when plotted, changes in the width of the container are represented by a curve and form a non-linear graph.","In this small step, students explore flow problems by comparing how different-shaped containers are filled or drained. Start by comparing the filling of containers in the shape of prisms or cylinders with identical heights but differing bases, highlighting the effect that the shape has on how quickly it fills. Show that the graphs related to the time taken and depth of liquid in each container form a straight line, as the rate of flow is constant. Move on to containers with non-parallel sides, for example a conical or boiling flask, to draw out that the depth of the water will increase more quickly when the container is narrower and more slowly when it is wider. Show that, when plotted, changes in the width of the container are represented by a curve and form a non-linear graph.","In this small step, students explore flow problems by comparing how different-shaped containers are filled or drained. Start by comparing the filling of containers in the shape of prisms or cylinders with identical heights but differing bases, highlighting the effect that the shape has on how quickly it fills. Sho