Year 9,13-14,Spring,,Maths and money,1.0,1,1091,Understand a bank account,Understand a bank account,"In this small step, students build on their Key Stage 2 maths and money skills, with new terminology introduced to enhance financial literacy through everyday examples. Introduce students to the layout and key features of a bank statement. Where possible, use genuine bank statements and household bills, as many banks provide sample statements online. Highlight how deposits and withdrawals affect the balance, reinforcing addition, subtraction and place value skills. Discuss credit balances and overdrawn balances, explaining how a positive balance means that money is available, while an overdrawn balance means that the account is in debt. Discuss direct debits and standing orders, explaining how regular payments appear on statements.","In this small step, students build on their Key Stage 2 maths and money skills, with new terminology introduced to enhance financial literacy through everyday examples. Introduce students to the layout and key features of a bank statement. Where possible, use genuine bank statements and household bills, as many banks provide sample statements online. Highlight how deposits and withdrawals affect the balance, reinforcing addition, subtraction and place value skills. Discuss credit balances and overdrawn balances, explaining how a positive balance means that money is available, while an overdrawn balance means that the account is in debt. Discuss direct debits and standing orders, explaining how regular payments appear on statements.","In this small step, students build on their Key Stage 2 maths and money skills, with new terminology introduced to enhance financial literacy through everyday examples. Introduce students to the layout and key features of a bank statement. Where possible, use genuine bank statements and household bills, as many banks provide sample statements online. Highlight how deposits and withdrawals affect the balance,