Year 9,13-14,Spring,,Maths and money,11.0,11,1101,Spending overseas,Spending overseas,"This small step focuses on the financial implications of spending overseas, with students exploring exchange rates, currency conversions and different payment options. Key considerations when travelling overseas are sales tax, the difference between paying with cash or card and phone charges. Compare paying in local currency with students’ own currency and how it affects the total cost, depending on bank and vendor exchange rates. Discuss how if no charge is set by the bank for paying in a different currency, then paying in local currency can be more cost effective, but it depends on the exchange rates that are set by both the bank and vendor. It can be helpful to highlight that British pounds (£), Great British pounds (GBP) and sterling all refer to the same currency. Students may see these terms used in different contexts, especially when comparing exchange rates or making payments abroad.","This small step focuses on the financial implications of spending overseas, with students exploring exchange rates, currency conversions and different payment options. Key considerations when travelling overseas are sales tax, the difference between paying with cash or card and phone charges. Compare paying in local currency with students’ own currency and how it affects the total cost, depending on bank and vendor exchange rates. Discuss how if no charge is set by the bank for paying in a different currency, then paying in local currency can be more cost effective, but it depends on the exchange rates that are set by both the bank and vendor. It can be helpful to highlight that British pounds (£), Great British pounds (GBP) and sterling all refer to the same currency. Students may see these terms used in different contexts, especially when comparing exchange rates or making payments abroad.","This small step focuses on the financial implications of spending overseas, with students exploring