Year 9,13-14,Spring,,Maths and money,12.0,12,1102,Insurance,Insurance,"In this small step, students continue to apply maths in a range of contexts as they explore insurance. Discuss different types of insurance in everyday life, for example car, travel, household and life insurance. Students examine the financial implications of insurance, including learning about insurance premium tax (IPT). IPT is applied to most general insurance policies, except life insurance. Students look at the standard 12% rate for home, car and pet insurance and the higher 20% rate for travel and mechanical repair insurance, such as boiler cover. They explore the idea of excess and consider how a lower premium does not always mean a better deal. It is also important to discuss how some types of insurance, such as car insurance, are mandatory, while others are optional and may depend on individual circumstances or financial situations.","In this small step, students continue to apply maths in a range of contexts as they explore insurance. Discuss different types of insurance in everyday life, for example car, travel, household and life insurance. Students examine the financial implications of insurance, including learning about insurance premium tax (IPT). IPT is applied to most general insurance policies, except life insurance. Students look at the standard 12% rate for home, car and pet insurance and the higher 20% rate for travel and mechanical repair insurance, such as boiler cover. They explore the idea of excess and consider how a lower premium does not always mean a better deal. It is also important to discuss how some types of insurance, such as car insurance, are mandatory, while others are optional and may depend on individual circumstances or financial situations.","In this small step, students continue to apply maths in a range of contexts as they explore insurance. Discuss different types of insurance in everyday life, for example car, travel, household and life insurance. Stud