Year 9,13-14,Spring,,Straight line graphs,1.0,1,1103,"Lines parallel to the axes, y = x and y = −x","Lines parallel to the axes, y = x and y = −x","In this small step, students revisit and extend learning of equations of lines parallel to the axes and the lines y = x and y = −x. Start with lines parallel to the axes before moving on to y = x and y = −x, ensuring that students can both plot and recognise the lines. It is important that they understand that the equation of a line describes a relationship between any pair of coordinates on that line. For example, any point on the line y = 3 will have a y-coordinate of 3. Similarly, any point on the line y = −x will have coordinates of the form (−a, a) or (a, −a). Draw attention to the x-axis being the line y = 0 and the y-axis being the line x = 0","In this small step, students revisit and extend learning of equations of lines parallel to the axes and the lines y = x and y = −x. Start with lines parallel to the axes before moving on to y = x and y = −x, ensuring that students can both plot and recognise the lines. It is important that they understand that the equation of a line describes a relationship between any pair of coordinates on that line. For example, any point on the line y = 3 will have a y-coordinate of 3. Similarly, any point on the line y = −x will have coordinates of the form (−a, a) or (a, −a). Draw attention to the x-axis being the line y = 0 and the y-axis being the line x = 0","In this small step, students revisit and extend learning of equations of lines parallel to the axes and the lines y = x and y = −x. Start with lines parallel to the axes before moving on to y = x and y = −x, ensuring that students can both plot and recognise the lines. It is important that they understand that the equation of a line describes a relationship between any pair of coordinates on that line. For example, any point on the line y = 3 will have a y-coordinate of 3. Similarly, any point on the line y = −x will have coor