Year 9,13-14,Spring,,Constructions and congruence,4.0,4,1120,Construct an angle bisector,Construct an angle bisector,"In this small step, students bisect an angle using a pair of compasses. Establish that bisecting an angle means that an angle is split into two equal angles. For example, bisecting a 60° angle results in two 30° angles. Once students are confident in bisecting a single angle, present them with other problems, for example bisecting an angle in a polygon. They should also be given opportunities to bisect angles in a given context, for example using an angle bisector to identify a region closer to one side than another in a polygon. Ensure that students keep all construction lines when constructing angle bisectors.","In this small step, students bisect an angle using a pair of compasses. Establish that bisecting an angle means that an angle is split into two equal angles. For example, bisecting a 60° angle results in two 30° angles. Once students are confident in bisecting a single angle, present them with other problems, for example bisecting an angle in a polygon. They should also be given opportunities to bisect angles in a given context, for example using an angle bisector to identify a region closer to one side than another in a polygon. Ensure that students keep all construction lines when constructing angle bisectors.","In this small step, students bisect an angle using a pair of compasses. Establish that bisecting an angle means that an angle is split into two equal angles. For example, bisecting a 60° angle results in two 30° angles. Once students are confident in bisecting a single angle, present them with other problems, for example bisecting an angle in a polygon. They should also be given opportunities to bisect angles in a given context, for example using an angle bisector to identify a region closer to one side than another in a polygon. Ensure that students keep all construction lines when constructing angle bisectors.",baseline,Year 9_1