Year 10,14-15,Autumn,Higher,Expand double brackets,5.0,5,1216,Solve quadratic equations equal to 0,Solve quadratic equations equal to 0,"In this small step, students will explore expressions in the form x2 – a2 where a is an integer. Encourage students to expand brackets of the form (x ± a)(x ± a) to uncover the difference of two squares. Explore the connection to quadratics in the form x2 ± bx ± c, noting how the middle term bx is eliminated. Highlight to students that, for example, in the expression x2 – 36, the value of b is 0, hence why it is omitted. Then encourage students to consider a factor pair of –36 that sum to 0, to enable them to factorise the expression. Repeat with other expressions in this form, encouraging students to spot that the value of c is always a square number. Once students are confident, challenge them to factorise expressions like 4x2 – 25 If appropriate, explore fully factorising expressions such as 48 – 3y2","In this small step, students will explore expressions in the form x2 – a2 where a is an integer. Encourage students to expand brackets of the form (x ± a)(x ± a) to uncover the difference of two squares. Explore the connection to quadratics in the form x2 ± bx ± c, noting how the middle term bx is eliminated. Highlight to students that, for example, in the expression x2 – 36, the value of b is 0, hence why it is omitted. Then encourage students to consider a factor pair of –36 that sum to 0, to enable them to factorise the expression. Repeat with other expressions in this form, encouraging students to spot that the value of c is always a square number. Once students are confident, challenge them to factorise expressions like 4x2 – 25 If appropriate, explore fully factorising expressions such as 48 – 3y2","In this small step, students will explore expressions in the form x2 – a2 where a is an integer. Encourage students to expand brackets of the form (x ± a)(x ± a) to uncover the difference of two squares. Explore the connection to