Year 10,14-15,Autumn,Foundation,Ratio and scale,1.0,1,1242,Equivalent ratios,Equivalent ratios,"In this small step, students find equivalent ratios and simplify ratios, building on their learning from Key Stage 3. Remind students to identify the highest common factor of the numbers in a given ratio and highlight that dividing by the highest common factor will give a ratio in its simplest form. Other strategies can also be explored, such as dividing the ratio 24 : 18 by 2, and then by 3. Ensure that students understand that equivalent ratios are multiples or factors of each other and discuss how to work out equivalent ratios, for example by doubling both numbers in a given ratio. Discuss with students how ratios involving units such as money, time or length need to be re-written using the same units before they can be simplified. If appropriate, students could explore writing ratios in the form 1 : n or n : 1. Note that this is an extend step in Year 8, so some students may not have covered this before.","In this small step, students find equivalent ratios and simplify ratios, building on their learning from Key Stage 3. Remind students to identify the highest common factor of the numbers in a given ratio and highlight that dividing by the highest common factor will give a ratio in its simplest form. Other strategies can also be explored, such as dividing the ratio 24 : 18 by 2, and then by 3. Ensure that students understand that equivalent ratios are multiples or factors of each other and discuss how to work out equivalent ratios, for example by doubling both numbers in a given ratio. Discuss with students how ratios involving units such as money, time or length need to be re-written using the same units before they can be simplified. If appropriate, students could explore writing ratios in the form 1 : n or n : 1. Note that this is an extend step in Year 8, so some students may not have covered this before.","In this small step, students find equivalent ratios and 