Year 10,14-15,Autumn,Higher,Ratio and scale,4.0,4,1249,Combine a set of ratios,Combine a set of ratios,"In this small step, students use their knowledge of equivalent ratios to combine two ratios. Remind students how to identify the lowest common multiple of two numbers and ensure that they can work with equivalent ratios. Support students by modelling the use of language such as “for every red, there are yellow”. In addition, encourage students to identify common parts between ratios. For example, the ratios red : blue and blue : yellow both include “blue”. Highlight that the common parts in ratios must be equal to combine them and explore methods for this, such as scaling one of the given ratios. Extra challenge can be added by changing the common term. For example, providing the ratios red : blue and red : yellow.","In this small step, students use their knowledge of equivalent ratios to combine two ratios. Remind students how to identify the lowest common multiple of two numbers and ensure that they can work with equivalent ratios. Support students by modelling the use of language such as “for every red, there are yellow”. In addition, encourage students to identify common parts between ratios. For example, the ratios red : blue and blue : yellow both include “blue”. Highlight that the common parts in ratios must be equal to combine them and explore methods for this, such as scaling one of the given ratios. Extra challenge can be added by changing the common term. For example, providing the ratios red : blue and red : yellow.","In this small step, students use their knowledge of equivalent ratios to combine two ratios. Remind students how to identify the lowest common multiple of two numbers and ensure that they can work with equivalent ratios. Support students by modelling the use of language such as “for every red, there are yellow”. In addition, encourage students to identify common parts between ratios. For example, the ratios red : blue and blue : yellow bo