Year 10,14-15,Autumn,Foundation,Work with fractions,2.0,2,1258,Increase or decrease an amount by a fraction,Increase or decrease an amount by a fraction,"In this small step, students build on their understanding of fractions of amounts to explore how to increase or decrease an amount by a fraction. Begin with calculations involving unit fractions, first finding the fraction of the amount before increasing or decreasing. As students develop confidence, progress to calculations with non- unit fractions. Bar models can illustrate the difference between the original and adjusted amounts, helping students clearly see fractional increases and decreases. This concept applies to real- world contexts, such as calculating a fractional discount or price increase. Encourage students to explore different methods, for example decreasing an amount by 3 5 can be found by calculating 2 5 of the amount. Similarly, equivalent percentages can be linked to increase and decrease problems.","In this small step, students build on their understanding of fractions of amounts to explore how to increase or decrease an amount by a fraction. Begin with calculations involving unit fractions, first finding the fraction of the amount before increasing or decreasing. As students develop confidence, progress to calculations with non- unit fractions. Bar models can illustrate the difference between the original and adjusted amounts, helping students clearly see fractional increases and decreases. This concept applies to real- world contexts, such as calculating a fractional discount or price increase. Encourage students to explore different methods, for example decreasing an amount by 3 5 can be found by calculating 2 5 of the amount. Similarly, equivalent percentages can be linked to increase and decrease problems.","In this small step, students build on their understanding of fractions of amounts to explore how to increase or decrease an amount by a fraction. Begin with calculations involving unit f