Year 10,14-15,Spring,Higher,Probability,4.0,4,1299,Relative frequency,Relative frequency,"In this small step, students consolidate their understanding from Key Stage 3, to consider relative frequency as a form of experimental probability. Students should recognise that probability is often considered in theoretical terms, where predictions are made about events that have not yet happened, while relative frequency is based on data from experiments or past events. Comparing theoretical probability with relative frequency can strengthen students’ understanding. A key discussion point is the effect of the number of trials on accuracy. Students should see that as the number of trials increases, the relative frequency tends to approach the theoretical probability. Students will also estimate the expected number of outcomes of an event using the relative frequency.","In this small step, students consolidate their understanding from Key Stage 3, to consider relative frequency as a form of experimental probability. Students should recognise that probability is often considered in theoretical terms, where predictions are made about events that have not yet happened, while relative frequency is based on data from experiments or past events. Comparing theoretical probability with relative frequency can strengthen students’ understanding. A key discussion point is the effect of the number of trials on accuracy. Students should see that as the number of trials increases, the relative frequency tends to approach the theoretical probability. Students will also estimate the expected number of outcomes of an event using the relative frequency.","In this small step, students consolidate their understanding from Key Stage 3, to consider relative frequency as a form of experimental probability. Students should recognise that probability is often considered in theoretical terms, where predictions are made about events that have not yet happened, while relative frequency is based on data 