Year 10,14-15,Spring,Higher,Probability,7.0,7,1305,Independent events,Independent events,"In this small step, students revisit independent events, first introduced in Key Stage 3. Distinguishing between independent and dependent events can be a challenging concept, so it is important to spend time ensuring students can recognise the difference. Independent events are those where the occurrence of one event does not affect the probability of the other. Once confident in identifying independent events, students apply the multiplication rule, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B), to calculate the probability of two or more independent events occurring. This can then be extended by using the rule to test whether two events are independent.","In this small step, students revisit independent events, first introduced in Key Stage 3. Distinguishing between independent and dependent events can be a challenging concept, so it is important to spend time ensuring students can recognise the difference. Independent events are those where the occurrence of one event does not affect the probability of the other. Once confident in identifying independent events, students apply the multiplication rule, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B), to calculate the probability of two or more independent events occurring. This can then be extended by using the rule to test whether two events are independent.","In this small step, students revisit independent events, first introduced in Key Stage 3. Distinguishing between independent and dependent events can be a challenging concept, so it is important to spend time ensuring students can recognise the difference. Independent events are those where the occurrence of one event does not affect the probability of the other. Once confident in identifying independent events, students apply the multiplication rule, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B), to calculate the probability of two or more independent events occurring. This can then be extended by using the rule to test whether tw