Year 10,14-15,Spring,Higher,Interpret and represent data,3.0,3,1335,Mean from a grouped frequency table,Mean from a grouped frequency table,"In this small step, students estimate the mean from grouped frequency tables. This may have previously been covered in Year 8 as an extend step. Begin by reviewing how to calculate the mean from raw data to highlight the difference when working with grouped data. Emphasise that when data is grouped, individual values are unknown, so the mean can only be estimated. Explain that the midpoint of each interval is used as a representative value, assuming an even distribution within each group. Model the process step by step, showing how to identify midpoints, multiply them by their corresponding frequencies, sum these products, and divide by the total frequency to find an estimated mean.","In this small step, students estimate the mean from grouped frequency tables. This may have previously been covered in Year 8 as an extend step. Begin by reviewing how to calculate the mean from raw data to highlight the difference when working with grouped data. Emphasise that when data is grouped, individual values are unknown, so the mean can only be estimated. Explain that the midpoint of each interval is used as a representative value, assuming an even distribution within each group. Model the process step by step, showing how to identify midpoints, multiply them by their corresponding frequencies, sum these products, and divide by the total frequency to find an estimated mean.","In this small step, students estimate the mean from grouped frequency tables. This may have previously been covered in Year 8 as an extend step. Begin by reviewing how to calculate the mean from raw data to highlight the difference when working with grouped data. Emphasise that when data is grouped, individual values are unknown, so the mean can only be estimated. Explain that the midpoint of each interval is used as a representative value, assuming an even distribut