Year 10,14-15,Spring,Foundation,Perimeter area and volume,6.0,6,1340,Circumference of a circle,Circumference of a circle,"In this small step, students use pi (π) to calculate the circumference of a circle. It may be useful to begin by asking students to measure both the circumference (C) and diameter (d) of various circles to investigate the relationship between the two, leading to the understanding that C d ≈ 3.14 This then leads to the discovery of the formula C = π × d. Students should practise applying this formula to find the circumference when given either the diameter or the radius. Students can also be challenged to work backwards, using a given circumference to determine the length of the diameter or radius. Encourage the use of the π button on calculators, rather than 3.14, to ensure greater accuracy. Although calculators may be used throughout this step, ensure students also practise expressing their answers in terms of π.","In this small step, students use pi (π) to calculate the circumference of a circle. It may be useful to begin by asking students to measure both the circumference (C) and diameter (d) of various circles to investigate the relationship between the two, leading to the understanding that C d ≈ 3.14 This then leads to the discovery of the formula C = π × d. Students should practise applying this formula to find the circumference when given either the diameter or the radius. Students can also be challenged to work backwards, using a given circumference to determine the length of the diameter or radius. Encourage the use of the π button on calculators, rather than 3.14, to ensure greater accuracy. Although calculators may be used throughout this step, ensure students also practise expressing their answers in terms of π.","In this small step, students use pi (π) to calculate the circumference of a circle. It may be useful to begin by asking students to measure both the circumference (C) and diameter (d) of various circles to investigate the 