Year 10,14-15,Spring,Higher,Interpret and represent data,6.0,6,1341,Types of data,Types of data,"In this small step, students revisit their Year 8 learning on discrete, continuous, quantitative and qualitative data, and explore the distinction between primary and secondary data. Those following the support scheme may not have previously encountered these data types, so it’s important to model clear examples and discuss each one to build understanding. Once students are confident, they can collect primary data through a simple survey or experiment, for example, measuring classmates’ heights or gathering information about their hobbies. Ask students to find secondary data on a related topic, such as national statistics or published survey results. Encourage them to discuss the advantages and disadvantages of each data source: primary data is usually more relevant but takes longer to collect, while secondary data is quicker to obtain but may be less specific or reliable.","In this small step, students revisit their Year 8 learning on discrete, continuous, quantitative and qualitative data, and explore the distinction between primary and secondary data. Those following the support scheme may not have previously encountered these data types, so it’s important to model clear examples and discuss each one to build understanding. Once students are confident, they can collect primary data through a simple survey or experiment, for example, measuring classmates’ heights or gathering information about their hobbies. Ask students to find secondary data on a related topic, such as national statistics or published survey results. Encourage them to discuss the advantages and disadvantages of each data source: primary data is usually more relevant but takes longer to collect, while secondary data is quicker to obtain but may be less specific or reliable.","In this small step, students revisit their Year 8 learning on discrete, continuous, quantitative and qualitative data, and expl