Year 10,14-15,Spring,Higher,Interpret and represent data,7.0,7,1343,Sampling,Sampling,"In this small step, students develop their understanding of data collection by exploring sampling, a method used to gather information from part of a population when it isn’t practical to survey everyone. Begin by defining the population as the entire group being studied, such as all students in a school, and a sample as a smaller group representing it. Introduce random sampling, where each individual has an equal chance of being selected, and systematic sampling, where selections are made at regular intervals. Once students are confident, explore stratified sampling, where the population is divided into groups (strata) and a proportional sample is taken from each. Encourage students to apply proportional reasoning to calculate sample sizes and discuss which method is most reliable in different situations. Emphasise the importance of avoiding bias when selecting samples.","In this small step, students develop their understanding of data collection by exploring sampling, a method used to gather information from part of a population when it isn’t practical to survey everyone. Begin by defining the population as the entire group being studied, such as all students in a school, and a sample as a smaller group representing it. Introduce random sampling, where each individual has an equal chance of being selected, and systematic sampling, where selections are made at regular intervals. Once students are confident, explore stratified sampling, where the population is divided into groups (strata) and a proportional sample is taken from each. Encourage students to apply proportional reasoning to calculate sample sizes and discuss which method is most reliable in different situations. Emphasise the importance of avoiding bias when selecting samples.","In this small step, students develop their understanding of data collection by exploring sampling, a method used to gather information from p