Year 10,14-15,Spring,Higher,Interpret and represent data,8.0,8,1345,Capture and recapture,Capture and recapture,"In this small step, students use proportional reasoning to estimate a population with the capture–recapture method. Using familiar examples, such as estimating the number of fish in a pond, helps make the idea relatable and emphasises why estimation is needed when counting every individual is impractical. Begin by discussing the key assumption that the proportion of marked individuals in the second sample reflects the proportion in the entire population. For instance, if 10 fish are marked initially and, in a second sample of 20 fish, 2 are marked, then 10 N = 2 20 . Have students solve to calculate N to see how the population estimate arises from proportional reasoning. Conclude by discussing when estimates may be unreliable, such as when marked fish don’t mix evenly, highlighting the importance of the method’s assumptions.","In this small step, students use proportional reasoning to estimate a population with the capture–recapture method. Using familiar examples, such as estimating the number of fish in a pond, helps make the idea relatable and emphasises why estimation is needed when counting every individual is impractical. Begin by discussing the key assumption that the proportion of marked individuals in the second sample reflects the proportion in the entire population. For instance, if 10 fish are marked initially and, in a second sample of 20 fish, 2 are marked, then 10 N = 2 20 . Have students solve to calculate N to see how the population estimate arises from proportional reasoning. Conclude by discussing when estimates may be unreliable, such as when marked fish don’t mix evenly, highlighting the importance of the method’s assumptions.","In this small step, students use proportional reasoning to estimate a population with the capture–recapture method. Using familiar examples, such as estimating the number of fish in a pond, helps make the idea