Year 10,14-15,Spring,Foundation,Perimeter area and volume,9.0,9,1346,Nets,Nets,"In this small step, students build on their prior knowledge from Key Stage 3 to explore the nets of 3-D shapes. They explore how 3-D shapes are formed and how each face corresponds to a familiar 2-D shape. Throughout this step, encourage students to use precise vocabulary such as face, edge and vertex. Begin with physical examples of nets for cubes and cuboids to show how the 2-D shapes fold to form a 3-D object. Use examples and non-examples to highlight that the faces must be arranged correctly for the net to fold together correctly. Matching activities can help students identify a 3-D shape from its net and vice versa. Challenge students to draw accurate nets of 3-D shapes when given specific dimensions.","In this small step, students build on their prior knowledge from Key Stage 3 to explore the nets of 3-D shapes. They explore how 3-D shapes are formed and how each face corresponds to a familiar 2-D shape. Throughout this step, encourage students to use precise vocabulary such as face, edge and vertex. Begin with physical examples of nets for cubes and cuboids to show how the 2-D shapes fold to form a 3-D object. Use examples and non-examples to highlight that the faces must be arranged correctly for the net to fold together correctly. Matching activities can help students identify a 3-D shape from its net and vice versa. Challenge students to draw accurate nets of 3-D shapes when given specific dimensions.","In this small step, students build on their prior knowledge from Key Stage 3 to explore the nets of 3-D shapes. They explore how 3-D shapes are formed and how each face corresponds to a familiar 2-D shape. Throughout this step, encourage students to use precise vocabulary such as face, edge and vertex. Begin with physical examples of nets for cubes and cuboids to show how the 2-D shapes fold to form a 3-D object. Use examples and non-examples to highlight that the faces must be