Year 10,14-15,Spring,Higher,Interpret and represent data,9.0,9,1347,Scatter graphs,Scatter graphs,"In this small step, students review scatter graphs, building on their prior knowledge from Key Stage 3. The focus is on reinforcing key vocabulary such as “positive correlation”, “negative correlation” and “no correlation”, and developing confidence in interpreting the relationship between two variables from the graph. Draw students’ attention to how the direction and strength of the pattern suggest different types of correlation. Students should also practise drawing a line of best fit and using it to make simple estimates. It is worth noting that, while lines of best fit in maths are straight lines, they may be curved in science depending on the nature of the data. It is valuable to remind students that correlation does not imply causality, and that the absence of a clear linear relationship does not necessarily mean the variables are unconnected.","In this small step, students review scatter graphs, building on their prior knowledge from Key Stage 3. The focus is on reinforcing key vocabulary such as “positive correlation”, “negative correlation” and “no correlation”, and developing confidence in interpreting the relationship between two variables from the graph. Draw students’ attention to how the direction and strength of the pattern suggest different types of correlation. Students should also practise drawing a line of best fit and using it to make simple estimates. It is worth noting that, while lines of best fit in maths are straight lines, they may be curved in science depending on the nature of the data. It is valuable to remind students that correlation does not imply causality, and that the absence of a clear linear relationship does not necessarily mean the variables are unconnected.","In this small step, students review scatter graphs, building on their prior knowledge from Key Stage 3. The focus is on reinforcing key vocabulary such as “positive correlat