Year 10,14-15,Spring,Foundation,Interpret and represent data,1.0,1,1350,Averages and range,Averages and range,"In this small step, students revisit their Year 8 knowledge by calculating averages and the range from a small data set. Start by reviewing how to calculate the mean, median, mode and range. Draw students’ attention to the fact that the range measures the spread of the data and is not an average. Emphasise that each type of average represents the data differently and encourage students to choose the most appropriate average based on the context, such as selecting the mode for categorical data. Highlight the importance of considering outliers and their impact on the averages. Once students are confident in calculating averages and the range, introduce more complex problems, such as finding an unknown value in a data set when given the mean, median, mode or range, or combinations of these.","In this small step, students revisit their Year 8 knowledge by calculating averages and the range from a small data set. Start by reviewing how to calculate the mean, median, mode and range. Draw students’ attention to the fact that the range measures the spread of the data and is not an average. Emphasise that each type of average represents the data differently and encourage students to choose the most appropriate average based on the context, such as selecting the mode for categorical data. Highlight the importance of considering outliers and their impact on the averages. Once students are confident in calculating averages and the range, introduce more complex problems, such as finding an unknown value in a data set when given the mean, median, mode or range, or combinations of these.","In this small step, students revisit their Year 8 knowledge by calculating averages and the range from a small data set. Start by reviewing how to calculate the mean, median, mode and range. Draw students’ attention to the fact that the range measures the spread of the data and is not a