Year 10,14-15,Spring,Higher,Non-linear graphs,3.0,3,1355,Turning points,Turning points,"In this small step, students build on their understanding of quadratic graphs and completing the square to explore turning points. Students can begin by identifying the coordinates of a turning point by inspection. Highlight that equations with a positive coefficient of x2 have a minimum turning point and that those with a negative coefficient of x2 have a maximum turning point. Draw attention to the link between the completed square form of the quadratic, y = (x + c)2 + d, and the coordinates of the turning point (–c, d). Once confident, students can complete the square to find turning points of graphs whose equations have negative x2 coefficients and those with x2 coefficients that are greater than 1. Dynamic graphing software can be used to support student understanding.","In this small step, students build on their understanding of quadratic graphs and completing the square to explore turning points. Students can begin by identifying the coordinates of a turning point by inspection. Highlight that equations with a positive coefficient of x2 have a minimum turning point and that those with a negative coefficient of x2 have a maximum turning point. Draw attention to the link between the completed square form of the quadratic, y = (x + c)2 + d, and the coordinates of the turning point (–c, d). Once confident, students can complete the square to find turning points of graphs whose equations have negative x2 coefficients and those with x2 coefficients that are greater than 1. Dynamic graphing software can be used to support student understanding.","In this small step, students build on their understanding of quadratic graphs and completing the square to explore turning points. Students can begin by identifying the coordinates of a turning point by inspection. Highlight that equations with a positive coefficient of x2 have a minimum turning point and that those with a negative coef