Year 10,14-15,Spring,Higher,Non-linear graphs,5.0,5,1359,Approximate solutions to equations using graphs,Approximate solutions to equations using graphs,"In this small step, students plot additional lines on graphs to find solutions to equations, building on their understanding of finding roots of quadratics from graphs. This step reinforces the equivalence of roots and solutions of equations in the form ax2 + bx + c = 0 Start by asking them to draw a given horizontal line in the form y = d alongside a graph of the form y = ax2 + bx + c to solve ax2 + bx + c = d. For example, the solutions of the equation x2 + 5x + 6 = 1 can be found by identifying the x-coordinates of the points where the graphs y = x2 + 5x + 6 and y = 1 intersect. Once students are confident, introduce further examples requiring lines of the form y = ax + b, supporting students to determine and plot the correct additional line. For example, adding a line of y = 2x + 4 to the graph of y = 2x2 + 3x + 1 to solve 2x2 + x – 3 = 0","In this small step, students plot additional lines on graphs to find solutions to equations, building on their understanding of finding roots of quadratics from graphs. This step reinforces the equivalence of roots and solutions of equations in the form ax2 + bx + c = 0 Start by asking them to draw a given horizontal line in the form y = d alongside a graph of the form y = ax2 + bx + c to solve ax2 + bx + c = d. For example, the solutions of the equation x2 + 5x + 6 = 1 can be found by identifying the x-coordinates of the points where the graphs y = x2 + 5x + 6 and y = 1 intersect. Once students are confident, introduce further examples requiring lines of the form y = ax + b, supporting students to determine and plot the correct additional line. For example, adding a line of y = 2x + 4 to the graph of y = 2x2 + 3x + 1 to solve 2x2 + x – 3 = 0","In this small step, students plot additional lines on graphs to find solutions to equations, building on their understanding of find