Year 10,14-15,Spring,Higher,Non-linear graphs,6.0,6,1361,Estimate the gradient of a curve at a given point,Estimate the gradient of a curve at a given point,"In this small step, students learn to draw tangents to curves and calculate an estimate of the gradient at a given point. They are introduced to the concept of a tangent as a straight line that touches a curve at a single point, without crossing it. Dynamic graphing software can help illustrate how a tangent at a point represents the gradient at that point. Model the process of drawing a tangent, guiding students to position their line as close as possible to the point, while keeping it equidistant from the curve on both sides. Once the tangent is drawn, students can calculate its gradient. Remind students that the gradient of the curve is an estimate unless the tangent at the given point is provided. Allow students to explore tangents to both quadratic and cubic graphs within this step.","In this small step, students learn to draw tangents to curves and calculate an estimate of the gradient at a given point. They are introduced to the concept of a tangent as a straight line that touches a curve at a single point, without crossing it. Dynamic graphing software can help illustrate how a tangent at a point represents the gradient at that point. Model the process of drawing a tangent, guiding students to position their line as close as possible to the point, while keeping it equidistant from the curve on both sides. Once the tangent is drawn, students can calculate its gradient. Remind students that the gradient of the curve is an estimate unless the tangent at the given point is provided. Allow students to explore tangents to both quadratic and cubic graphs within this step.","In this small step, students learn to draw tangents to curves and calculate an estimate of the gradient at a given point. They are introduced to the concept of a tangent as a straight line that touches a curve at a single point, without cro