Year 10,14-15,Spring,Higher,Non-calculator methods,2.0,2,1370,Related calculations,Related calculations,"In this small step, students use known numerical facts to derive other related facts. Students will make links between similar calculations, for example, understanding that if 12 × 9 = 108 then 1.2 × 0.9 = 1.08. Attention should be drawn to how the numbers in a calculation have been manipulated and the effect this will have on a given answer. For example, if both numbers in a multiplication are multiplied by 10, then the new product will be 100 times greater than the original product. Students must also understand that this logic does not apply to division. For example, if both numbers in a division are multiplied by 10, then the new product will be the same as the original product. For division, this can be linked to students’ understanding of equivalent fractions, for example, 45 ÷ 0.5 = 45 0.5 = 90 1 = 90","In this small step, students use known numerical facts to derive other related facts. Students will make links between similar calculations, for example, understanding that if 12 × 9 = 108 then 1.2 × 0.9 = 1.08. Attention should be drawn to how the numbers in a calculation have been manipulated and the effect this will have on a given answer. For example, if both numbers in a multiplication are multiplied by 10, then the new product will be 100 times greater than the original product. Students must also understand that this logic does not apply to division. For example, if both numbers in a division are multiplied by 10, then the new product will be the same as the original product. For division, this can be linked to students’ understanding of equivalent fractions, for example, 45 ÷ 0.5 = 45 0.5 = 90 1 = 90","In this small step, students use known numerical facts to derive other related facts. Students will make links between similar calculations, for example, understanding that if 12 × 9 = 108 then 1.2 × 0.9 = 1.08. Attention should be drawn to how the nu