Year 10,14-15,Spring,Foundation,Non-linear graphs,2.0,2,1381,Intercepts and roots of quadratic graphs (E),Intercepts and roots of quadratic graphs (E),"In this small step, students identify and interpret the roots and intercepts of quadratic graphs, reinforcing connections to their previous work on solving quadratics. Begin by exploring examples where the roots and y-intercept can be identified through inspection before introducing algebraic methods. Emphasise that the roots of the equation y = ax2 + bx + c correspond to the solutions of ax2 + bx + c = 0 and should be written in the form x = d. Remind students that the y-intercept can be found by substituting x = 0 into the equation to identify the value of y before writing as a coordinate in the form (0, y). Provide opportunities for students to explore graphs that have 0, 1 or 2 roots, helping them recognise how the number of solutions relates to the graph’s intersection with the x-axis. Dynamic graphing software can be used to support understanding.","In this small step, students identify and interpret the roots and intercepts of quadratic graphs, reinforcing connections to their previous work on solving quadratics. Begin by exploring examples where the roots and y-intercept can be identified through inspection before introducing algebraic methods. Emphasise that the roots of the equation y = ax2 + bx + c correspond to the solutions of ax2 + bx + c = 0 and should be written in the form x = d. Remind students that the y-intercept can be found by substituting x = 0 into the equation to identify the value of y before writing as a coordinate in the form (0, y). Provide opportunities for students to explore graphs that have 0, 1 or 2 roots, helping them recognise how the number of solutions relates to the graph’s intersection with the x-axis. Dynamic graphing software can be used to support understanding.","In this small step, students identify and interpret the roots and intercepts of quadratic graphs, reinforcing co