Year 11,15-16,Autumn,Higher,Area and volume,3.0,3,1502,Volume of cones and pyramids,Volume of cones and pyramids,"In this small step, students calculate the volume of cones and pyramids. Students may not have covered this before, as this is an extend step in Year 9. When introducing the volume of a cone, it may be useful to draw out similarities with the formula for the volume of a cylinder. That is, the volume of a cone is 1 3 of the volume of a cylinder with the same height and radius. Then further similarity may be shown between the formulae for cones and pyramids. For example, the volume of both can be calculated with 1 3 × area of base × height. Provide opportunities for students to work backwards from a given volume to find dimensions of the cone or pyramid. If appropriate, explore problems where using Pythagoras’ theorem may be necessary to calculate the perpendicular height of the cone or pyramid.","In this small step, students calculate the volume of cones and pyramids. Students may not have covered this before, as this is an extend step in Year 9. When introducing the volume of a cone, it may be useful to draw out similarities with the formula for the volume of a cylinder. That is, the volume of a cone is 1 3 of the volume of a cylinder with the same height and radius. Then further similarity may be shown between the formulae for cones and pyramids. For example, the volume of both can be calculated with 1 3 × area of base × height. Provide opportunities for students to work backwards from a given volume to find dimensions of the cone or pyramid. If appropriate, explore problems where using Pythagoras’ theorem may be necessary to calculate the perpendicular height of the cone or pyramid.","In this small step, students calculate the volume of cones and pyramids. Students may not have covered this before, as this is an extend step in Year 9. When introducing the volume of a cone, it may be useful to draw out similarities with the formula for the volume of a c