Year 11,15-16,Autumn,Higher,Area and volume,6.0,6,1508,Convert metric units of area and volume,Convert metric units of area and volume,"In this small step, students convert metric units of area and volume. This may have been previously studied in the extend step in Year 9. Initially, explore the area of a square 1 cm in length and compare it to the area of a square 10 mm in length, to emphasise how 1 cm² is equal to 100 mm². A similar approach can be used to convert units of volume using a 1 cm cube compared to the volume of a 10 mm cube. Students should recognise that the area scale factor is the square of the linear scale factor, and the volume scale factor is the cube of the linear scale factor. Students can then progress to converting between units in a single calculation. Links between other measurements of volume such as m3 and cm3 should also be explored.","In this small step, students convert metric units of area and volume. This may have been previously studied in the extend step in Year 9. Initially, explore the area of a square 1 cm in length and compare it to the area of a square 10 mm in length, to emphasise how 1 cm² is equal to 100 mm². A similar approach can be used to convert units of volume using a 1 cm cube compared to the volume of a 10 mm cube. Students should recognise that the area scale factor is the square of the linear scale factor, and the volume scale factor is the cube of the linear scale factor. Students can then progress to converting between units in a single calculation. Links between other measurements of volume such as m3 and cm3 should also be explored.","In this small step, students convert metric units of area and volume. This may have been previously studied in the extend step in Year 9. Initially, explore the area of a square 1 cm in length and compare it to the area of a square 10 mm in length, to emphasise how 1 cm² is equal to 100 mm². A similar approach can be used to convert units of volume using a 1 cm cube compared to t