Year 11,15-16,Autumn,Foundation,Standard form,1.0,1,1560,Numbers greater than 1 in standard form,Numbers greater than 1 in standard form,"In this small step, students recap and consolidate their knowledge of standard form from Key Stage 3. It may be appropriate to teach this step and the next step in tandem. They will revisit converting numbers to and from standard form, solving problems and comparing or ordering numbers in standard form. Students that have exclusively followed the support scheme will not have converted from standard form to ordinary numbers. Students may need reminding of the correct format A × 10n where 1 ≤ A < 10 and n is an integer. Students need to able to recognise when a number is in standard form; using non-examples such as 12.6 × 105 is encouraged. Place value charts can be used to support students’ deeper understanding of standard form. Using numbers in context, such as distances in the solar system, helps to highlight why standard form is an efficient way to represent very large numbers.","In this small step, students recap and consolidate their knowledge of standard form from Key Stage 3. It may be appropriate to teach this step and the next step in tandem. They will revisit converting numbers to and from standard form, solving problems and comparing or ordering numbers in standard form. Students that have exclusively followed the support scheme will not have converted from standard form to ordinary numbers. Students may need reminding of the correct format A × 10n where 1 ≤ A < 10 and n is an integer. Students need to able to recognise when a number is in standard form; using non-examples such as 12.6 × 105 is encouraged. Place value charts can be used to support students’ deeper understanding of standard form. Using numbers in context, such as distances in the solar system, helps to highlight why standard form is an efficient way to represent very large numbers.","In this small step, students recap and consolidate their knowledge of st