Year 11,15-16,Spring,Higher,Transformations,1.0,1,1646,Enlargement (positive scale factor),Enlargement (positive scale factor),"In this small step, students enlarge shapes using positive integer scale factors, building on their understanding of similarity. Start with enlargements on a grid and highlight that scale factors greater than 1 produce larger images. As students progress, introduce enlargements with a given centre of enlargement. Encourage students to count distances from the centre of enlargement to each vertex of the shape and multiply it by the scale factor to determine the corresponding vertices of the image. Provide practice using centres that are not at the origin. Students should also work out the scale factor of an enlargement, and identify the centre of enlargement by drawing ray lines through the corresponding vertices of the object and image. The point where the ray lines meet is the centre of enlargement. Encourage students to use a ruler for accuracy and extend lines beyond the image if necessary.","In this small step, students enlarge shapes using positive integer scale factors, building on their understanding of similarity. Start with enlargements on a grid and highlight that scale factors greater than 1 produce larger images. As students progress, introduce enlargements with a given centre of enlargement. Encourage students to count distances from the centre of enlargement to each vertex of the shape and multiply it by the scale factor to determine the corresponding vertices of the image. Provide practice using centres that are not at the origin. Students should also work out the scale factor of an enlargement, and identify the centre of enlargement by drawing ray lines through the corresponding vertices of the object and image. The point where the ray lines meet is the centre of enlargement. Encourage students to use a ruler for accuracy and extend lines beyond the image if necessary.","In this small step, students enlarge shapes using posi