Year 11,15-16,Spring,Higher,Transformations,3.0,3,1650,Enlargement (negative scale factor),Enlargement (negative scale factor),"In this small step, students build on their understanding of enlargement by using negative scale factors. Begin with scale factors of –2 and –3, with a marked centre of enlargement on a grid. Guide them to count distances from the centre to each vertex, multiplying these distances by the scale factor to locate the corresponding vertices of the image. Explain to students that, unlike positive enlargements, applying a negative scale factor means the distance from the centre of enlargement is measured in the opposite direction. Highlight that a negative scale factor rotates the image 180° while also changing its size. As students develop, encourage them to apply this concept on a coordinate grid, where the centre of enlargement is given as a pair of coordinates. To deepen understanding, ensure students practise enlargements from various centres, including those away from the origin.","In this small step, students build on their understanding of enlargement by using negative scale factors. Begin with scale factors of –2 and –3, with a marked centre of enlargement on a grid. Guide them to count distances from the centre to each vertex, multiplying these distances by the scale factor to locate the corresponding vertices of the image. Explain to students that, unlike positive enlargements, applying a negative scale factor means the distance from the centre of enlargement is measured in the opposite direction. Highlight that a negative scale factor rotates the image 180° while also changing its size. As students develop, encourage them to apply this concept on a coordinate grid, where the centre of enlargement is given as a pair of coordinates. To deepen understanding, ensure students practise enlargements from various centres, including those away from the origin.","In this small step, students build on their understanding of enlargement by using 