Year 11,15-16,Spring,Higher,Transformations,9.0,9,1662,Invariant points (E),Invariant points (E),"In this small step, students develop their understanding of invariant points and lines by observing which parts of a shape remain unchanged during different transformations. Begin by demonstrating a reflection where the shape touches the mirror line, and ask students to identify points that have not moved. Highlight to students that points on the mirror line are invariant. Repeat with other transformations, highlighting that invariant points occur in rotations only when the centre lies on a vertex or edge of the shape. Similarly, in enlargements, this occurs only when the centre of enlargement is on the shape itself. When discussing enlargements, note special cases such as a scale factor of 1, where all points are invariant. Emphasise that translations have no invariant points, as every point moves the same distance and direction, unless the shape remains unchanged.","In this small step, students develop their understanding of invariant points and lines by observing which parts of a shape remain unchanged during different transformations. Begin by demonstrating a reflection where the shape touches the mirror line, and ask students to identify points that have not moved. Highlight to students that points on the mirror line are invariant. Repeat with other transformations, highlighting that invariant points occur in rotations only when the centre lies on a vertex or edge of the shape. Similarly, in enlargements, this occurs only when the centre of enlargement is on the shape itself. When discussing enlargements, note special cases such as a scale factor of 1, where all points are invariant. Emphasise that translations have no invariant points, as every point moves the same distance and direction, unless the shape remains unchanged.","In this small step, students develop their understanding of invariant points and lines by observing which parts of a shape remain unchanged 