Year 11,15-16,Spring,Higher,Rates,1.0,1,1665,"Speed, distance and time","Speed, distance and time","In this small step, students consolidate and build upon their understanding of problems involving speed, distance and time covered in Key Stage 3. Remind students that units of speed represent the distance covered for each unit of time, for example “miles per hour”. Students can be provided with formulae linking speed, distance and time, but should be encouraged to use a logical approach to solve problems related to speed, distance and time. For example, if the time is doubled, then the distance covered will also double if the speed is constant. Students could also inspect units given in a problem to decide on a strategy to solve it. For example, a speed in m/s can be calculated by dividing the distance (metres) by the time (seconds).","In this small step, students consolidate and build upon their understanding of problems involving speed, distance and time covered in Key Stage 3. Remind students that units of speed represent the distance covered for each unit of time, for example “miles per hour”. Students can be provided with formulae linking speed, distance and time, but should be encouraged to use a logical approach to solve problems related to speed, distance and time. For example, if the time is doubled, then the distance covered will also double if the speed is constant. Students could also inspect units given in a problem to decide on a strategy to solve it. For example, a speed in m/s can be calculated by dividing the distance (metres) by the time (seconds).","In this small step, students consolidate and build upon their understanding of problems involving speed, distance and time covered in Key Stage 3. Remind students that units of speed represent the distance covered for each unit of time, for example “miles per hour”. Students can be provided with formulae linking speed, distance and time, but should be encouraged to use a logical approach to solve proble