Year 11,15-16,Spring,Higher,Rates,2.0,2,1667,Distance-time graphs,Distance-time graphs,"In this small step, students link their understanding of speed, distance and time, by drawing and interpreting distance–time graphs. Provide opportunities for students to identify and discuss key features of the graphs, such as a horizontal line segment representing where an object is stationary, or a diagonal line segment representing an object moving at a constant speed. When modelling how to draw a distance–time graph, provide information related to time and distance, for example “a car travelled 40 miles in 1 hour”, before moving on to problems involving speed to work out a time/distance, for example “a cyclist travels at a speed of 50 km/h for 30 minutes”. If appropriate, students could calculate the average speed of a journey whilst moving, by considering the total distance travelled and the total time taken whilst moving.","In this small step, students link their understanding of speed, distance and time, by drawing and interpreting distance–time graphs. Provide opportunities for students to identify and discuss key features of the graphs, such as a horizontal line segment representing where an object is stationary, or a diagonal line segment representing an object moving at a constant speed. When modelling how to draw a distance–time graph, provide information related to time and distance, for example “a car travelled 40 miles in 1 hour”, before moving on to problems involving speed to work out a time/distance, for example “a cyclist travels at a speed of 50 km/h for 30 minutes”. If appropriate, students could calculate the average speed of a journey whilst moving, by considering the total distance travelled and the total time taken whilst moving.","In this small step, students link their understanding of speed, distance and time, by drawing and interpreting distance–time graphs. Provide opportunities for students to identify and discuss key features of the graphs, such a