Year 11,15-16,Spring,Higher,Rates,3.0,3,1669,Velocity-time graphs (E),Velocity-time graphs (E),"In this small step, students interpret velocity–time or speed– time graphs. Ensure that students understand that velocity is a measure of speed in a given direction, whereas speed does not indicate the direction. Students will learn that the gradient at a specific time on a velocity–time graph represents a change in velocity (acceleration). Begin with graphs involving only straight lines, highlighting that positive and negative gradients represent constant acceleration and deceleration, respectively. For curved graphs, model how to draw and calculate the gradient of a tangent at a point, discussing why the accuracy of a tangent may affect answers. Note that students will only look at motion in a single direction, therefore “velocity” and “speed” can be used interchangeably.","In this small step, students interpret velocity–time or speed– time graphs. Ensure that students understand that velocity is a measure of speed in a given direction, whereas speed does not indicate the direction. Students will learn that the gradient at a specific time on a velocity–time graph represents a change in velocity (acceleration). Begin with graphs involving only straight lines, highlighting that positive and negative gradients represent constant acceleration and deceleration, respectively. For curved graphs, model how to draw and calculate the gradient of a tangent at a point, discussing why the accuracy of a tangent may affect answers. Note that students will only look at motion in a single direction, therefore “velocity” and “speed” can be used interchangeably.","In this small step, students interpret velocity–time or speed– time graphs. Ensure that students understand that velocity is a measure of speed in a given direction, whereas speed does not indicate the direction. Students will learn that the gradient at a specific time on a velocity–time graph represents a change in velocity (ac