Year 11,15-16,Spring,Foundation,Transformations,7.0,7,1675,Describe a transformation,Describe a transformation,"In this small step, students learn to describe any transformation accurately, building on their learning from previous steps. Students begin by identifying the type of transformation: translation, reflection, rotation or enlargement. Encourage students to observe the differences between the object and the image, and consider whether the shape is in the same orientation (translation), mirrored (reflection), rotated (rotation) or changed size (enlargement). Students should focus on key properties such as size and orientation, to rule out incorrect options. Tracing paper can be helpful for physically testing transformations. Stem sentences can also support students in structuring their responses.","In this small step, students learn to describe any transformation accurately, building on their learning from previous steps. Students begin by identifying the type of transformation: translation, reflection, rotation or enlargement. Encourage students to observe the differences between the object and the image, and consider whether the shape is in the same orientation (translation), mirrored (reflection), rotated (rotation) or changed size (enlargement). Students should focus on key properties such as size and orientation, to rule out incorrect options. Tracing paper can be helpful for physically testing transformations. Stem sentences can also support students in structuring their responses.","In this small step, students learn to describe any transformation accurately, building on their learning from previous steps. Students begin by identifying the type of transformation: translation, reflection, rotation or enlargement. Encourage students to observe the differences between the object and the image, and consider whether the shape is in the same orientation (translation), mirrored (reflection), rotated (rotation) or changed size (enlargement). Students should fo