Year 7,11-12,Summer,,Angles and polygons,1.0,1,884,Draw and measure lines and angles,Draw and measure lines and angles,"In this small step, students develop the practical skills of drawing and measuring line segments and angles accurately, building on understanding from Key Stage 2 Recap the correct use of a ruler for drawing and measuring line segments before introducing the protractor as the tool for measuring and drawing angles. Ensure that students know how to align the baseline and select the correct scale, modelling these skills clearly. Emphasise that an angle is a measure of turn. Provide questions where students both draw given angles and measure angles already drawn. Encourage them to estimate before measuring in order to develop a sense of size. Everyday contexts, such as design or navigation, help to show why accuracy matters. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may misalign the protractor baseline with the line segment, leading to inaccurate measurements. • The term “line segment” may be unfamiliar and students may not realise that a line is infinite in length. • Students may read from the wrong scale on the protractor, for example 140° instead of 40°. • Students may misalign the ruler by not positioning the beginning of a line segment at zero. Year 7 | Summer term | Block 4 – Angles and polygons | Step 1 Mathematical talk • What is the difference between a line and a line segment? • When measuring a line segment, what happens if you do not start from zero on the ruler? • How can you measure the size of a turn? • How can you describe the direction of a turn? • How can you check that your protractor is lined up correctly before measuring? • Which scale should you read from the protractor? What strategies can you use to make sure that you are using the correct protractor scale? • Is the angle acute, right, obtuse or reflex? Explain how you know without measuring. • How would you use a protractor to measure an angle greater than 180°? National Curr