Year 7,11-12,Summer,,Angles and polygons,10.0,10,893,Parallel and perpendicular lines,Parallel and perpendicular lines,"In this small step, students consolidate and extend their Key Stage 2 understanding of parallel and perpendicular lines. Remind students that parallel lines are always the same distance apart and never meet, no matter how far they are extended. Perpendicular lines meet at a right angle (90°), which can be shown using a square corner or set square. Use clear diagrams and practical examples, such as train tracks for parallel lines or the corner of a book for perpendicular lines. Encourage students to spot these relationships in shapes, coordinate grids and the classroom environment. Emphasise accurate vocabulary, such as “intersect” and “right angle”, and correct notation, including arrows for parallel lines and squares for right angles. Students should practise drawing parallel and perpendicular lines using a ruler and protractor or set square. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may assume that all lines that do not meet are parallel, overlooking the fact that parallel lines must also be equidistant. • Students may assume that lines need to be the same length to be parallel. • Students may confuse perpendicular lines with any intersecting lines. Year 7 | Summer term | Block 4 – Angles and polygons | Step 10 Mathematical talk • How can you tell that two lines are parallel? • What does it mean for two lines to be the same distance apart? • Can parallel lines ever meet? Explain your answer. • What makes a pair of lines perpendicular? • How can you check that two lines are perpendicular? • What is the same and what is different about parallel lines and perpendicular lines? • Where might you see parallel/perpendicular lines in everyday life? • If line AB is perpendicular to CD, and EF is parallel to AB, what is the relationship between EF and CD? National Curriculum links • Describe, sketch and draw using conventional terms and notations: points