Year 8,12-13,Summer,,Tables and probability,9.0,9,1019,Probabilities from two-way tables,Probabilities from two-way tables,"In this small step, students use two-way tables to calculate probabilities. Model how to extract relevant information from a two-way table to work out probabilities. Emphasise the importance of carefully reading the question and identifying the correct values, highlighting which total to divide by, as this may be the total for the entire table or a specific row or column total, depending on the wording and focus of the question. Reinforce accurate probability notation and remind students to express their answers as fractions, decimals or percentages. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may misread the table and select the wrong cell for an outcome. • Students may use the number of times an outcome does not happen as the denominator of a fraction when calculating probability, rather than using the total number of outcomes. • Students may use the total from the whole table when they should use the sub-category total. Year 8 | Summer term | Block 2 – Tables and probability | Step 9 National Curriculum links • Record, describe and analyse the frequency of outcomes of simple probability experiments involving randomness, fairness, equally and unequally likely outcomes, using appropriate language and the 0–1 probability scale Mathematical talk • How can you use a two-way table to calculate a probability? • To find the probability of an event from a two-way table, do you always use the whole total as the denominator? Explain your answer. • What part of the table do you need to look at to answer the question? • What is the difference between the individual values and the totals? • How do you know if you should divide by the total from a row, the total from a column or the total of the whole table? • How do you decide which row or column to look at? • When expressing the probability as a fraction, what value is used for the denominator?","In this