Year 8,12-13,Summer,,Tables and probability,10.0,10,1020,Frequency trees,Frequency trees,"In this small step, students are introduced to frequency trees as a tool for organising and interpreting data. Model how to work out the values in a partially completed frequency tree, before students independently construct one from given information. Ensure that they understand how each part of the tree branches from the previous total. Use familiar contexts that involve two-stage groupings, such as splitting by year group and then by subject. Make clear links with part-whole models to support conceptual understanding. Students should recognise that the sum of values on any pair of branches must equal the total from the previous branch. Reinforce the importance of clear and consistent labelling and encourage students to check totals at each stage to ensure accuracy. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may forget that the values on branches at any stage must add up to the total from the previous node. • Students may not label branches clearly. • Students may assume that the order of branching does not matter. Year 8 | Summer term | Block 2 – Tables and probability | Step 10 National Curriculum links • Construct and interpret appropriate tables, charts, and diagrams, including frequency tables, bar charts, pie charts, and pictograms for categorical data, and vertical line (or bar) charts for ungrouped and grouped numerical data Mathematical talk • What does each branch on the frequency tree represent? • What are the two categories at each stage? • How can you label each branch to make your frequency tree easy to read? • Where can you find the total number of people/items in the diagram? • Explain the relationships between the numbers in a frequency tree. • How does the frequency tree show a part-whole relationship? • When might you have a frequency tree with more than two branches? • Can two different frequency trees represent the same scenario? Explain your answer. • H