Year 9,13-14,Summer,,Sets and probability,9.0,9,1157,Independent events,Independent events,"In this small step, students explore independent events. It can be helpful to introduce the idea through a sample space diagram, for example for rolling two dice, where all possible outcomes can be listed and favourable cases counted. Draw students’ attention to how this method produces the same result as multiplying the separate probabilities, for example 1 2 × 1 6 = 1 12 for rolling an even number on one dice and a 5 on the other. Making this connection explains why multiplication is used for independent events and helps to link different approaches. Encourage students to express probabilities as both fractions and decimals, supporting fluency and making comparisons easier. Using diagrams provides opportunities for students to develop accurate reasoning. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may think that “independent” means that the probabilities are equal, rather than that one outcome does not affect the other. • Students may confuse independent with mutually exclusive events, not recognising that mutually exclusive outcomes cannot happen together. National Curriculum links • Generate theoretical sample spaces for single and combined events with equally likely, mutually exclusive outcomes and use these to calculate theoretical probabilities Mathematical talk • What does “independent event” mean? • Give an example of two events that are independent. • Can two events be independent if one is much more likely than the other? • If I roll one dice and you roll another, does my result affect yours? Explain your answer. • How is independence different from fairness? • Can an event be unfair but still independent? Give an example. • Can two independent events happen at the same time? Explain your answer. • Can two mutually exclusive events happen at the same time? Explain your answer. • How does this difference change how you calculate their probabilities? • If the probability of winning a game is 0.3, what is the probability of winning twice in a row? Independent events Year 9 | Summer term | Block 3 – Sets and probability | Step 9","In this small step, students explore independent events. It can be helpful to introduce the idea through a sample space diagram, for example for rolling two dice, where all possible outcomes can be listed and favourable cases counted. Draw students’ attention to how this method produces the same result as multiplying the separate probabilities, for example 1 2 × 1 6 = 1 12 for rolling an even number on one dice and a 5 on the other. Making this connection explains why multiplication is used for independent events and helps to link different approaches. Encourage students to express probabilities as both fractions and decimals, supporting fluency and making comparisons easier. Using diagrams provides opportunities for students to develop accurate reasoning. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may think that “independent” means that the probabilities are equal, rather than that one outcome does not affect the other. • Students may confuse independent with mutually exclusive events, not recognising that mutually exclusive outcomes cannot happen together. National Curriculum links • Generate theoretical sample spaces for single and combined events with equally likely, mutually exclusive outcomes and use these to calculate theoretical probabilities Mathematical talk • What does “independent event” mean? • Give an example of two events that are independent. • Can two events be independent if one is much more likely than the other? • If I roll one dice and you roll another, does my result affect yours? Explain your answer. • How is independence different from fairness? • Can an event be unfair but still independent? Give an example. • Can two independent events happen at the same time? Explain your answer. • Can two mutually exclusive events happen at the same time? Explain your answer. • How does this