Year 9,13-14,Summer,,Sets and probability,10.0,10,1158,Probabilities from Venn diagrams,Probabilities from Venn diagrams,"In this small step, students explore probabilities using Venn diagrams. Recap the regions of a Venn diagram, clarifying how each represents a distinct group within the data. Draw students’ attention to how probabilities are calculated, by comparing the number of outcomes in a region with the total represented by the diagram. If appropriate, students could explore the “given that” clause, finding the probability of one event when another is already known to have occurred. This introduces conditional probability in a structured way and highlights how Venn diagrams make relationships between events visual. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may omit elements written outside the circles. • Students may forget to include the intersection when calculating the probability of “A or B”. • Students may think that the total in the denominator should be the sum of the two circles, rather than the overall total. • When answering “given that” questions, students may use the total population instead of restricting it to the given condition. National Curriculum links • Enumerate sets and unions/intersections of sets systematically, using tables, grids and Venn diagrams Mathematical talk • What does each circle represent? • What does the intersection represent? • What is the universal set? • What does the region outside the circles represent? • How can you tell from the Venn diagram which elements are prime numbers? • How can you tell from the Venn diagram how many elements there are in total? • When calculating a probability, what does the numerator represent? What does the denominator represent? • How do the words “and” and “or” relate to set notation and regions on a Venn diagram? • What is meant by the probability of A given that B has happened? Which region represents the numerator/ denominator? Probabilities from Venn diagrams Year 9 | Summer term 