Year 9,13-14,Summer,,Transformations,9.0,9,1167,Describe a translation,Describe a translation,"In this small step, students describe a translation using a column vector. Encourage students to identify a pair of corresponding vertices, then model how to calculate the horizontal and vertical movements needed to map one onto the other. Reinforce that the order is important, with the horizontal movement being written as the top value in the vector and the vertical movement below. Emphasise that the same vector applies to every vertex of the shape, not just the pair chosen. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may use words instead of a column vector. • Students may incorrectly write the column vector as a coordinate or fraction. • Students may write the column vector incorrectly, for example ( 2 3 ) rather than ( 3 2 ) , or ( 5 2 ) instead of (−5 −2) . • Students may describe a translation in the wrong order, for example describing the transformation of shape B to shape A, rather than shape A to shape B. • Students may struggle to interpret translations with column vectors of the form ( 0 a ) or ( b 0 ) . National Curriculum links • Identify properties of, and describe the results of, translations, rotations and reflections applied to given figures Mathematical talk • How can you describe the translation in words? How can you write this as a column vector? • Do you need any more information to describe a translation? • Which value in a column vector describes the horizontal movement? • Which value in a column vector describes the vertical movement? • How do you write a translation of 2 units left as a column vector? • How do you write a translation of 3 units down as a column vector? • If the translation vector from shape A to shape B is , what is the translation vector from shape B to shape A? • How can you check that your description is accurate? Describe a translation Year 9 | Summer term | Block 4 – Transformations | Step 9","In this small step, students desc