Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Angles,3.0,3,1399,Exterior angles of a polygon,Exterior angles of a polygon,"In this small step, students recap their prior learning of interior and exterior angles from Key Stage 3. Remind them that the sum of the exterior angles of any polygon is 360° and use this to calculate individual exterior angles in regular polygons by dividing 360° by the number of sides. Highlight that interior and exterior angles sum to 180°, and use this to calculate unknown exterior angles given an interior angle. Students can then extend this knowledge to find the number of sides of a regular polygon when either the interior or exterior angle is known. Encourage students to clearly label diagrams to support reasoning, and to help identify when sides or angles are equal. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may believe that the exterior and interior angles sum to 360°. • Students may believe that the sum of exterior angles depends on the number of sides the polygon has. • Students may assume that some equal angles always mean the polygon is regular, without checking that all sides are also equal in length. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 1 – Angles | Step 3 Mathematical talk • How can you identify the exterior angles of a polygon? • What is the relationship between an interior and an exterior angle in a polygon? • How can you determine if a polygon is regular? • What is the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon? • What is the formula to calculate the size of one exterior angle in a regular polygon? • What is the size of each exterior angle in a regular decagon? Explain how you know. • A regular polygon has exterior angles of 40°. How many sides does the polygon have? What is the mathematical name of the polygon? • An irregular pentagon has exterior angles of 82°, 102°, 37° and 90°. What is the size of the fifth exterior angle? H National Curriculum links • Derive and use the sum of angles in a triangle and use it to deduce the angle sum in any polygon and to derive properties of regular polygons (KS3)","In this small step, students recap their prior learning of interior and exterior angles from Key Stage 3. Remind them that the sum of the exterior angles of any polygon is 360° and use this to calculate individual exterior angles in regular polygons by dividing 360° by the number of sides. Highlight that interior and exterior angles sum to 180°, and use this to calculate unknown exterior angles given an interior angle. Students can then extend this knowledge to find the number of sides of a regular polygon when either the interior or exterior angle is known. Encourage students to clearly label diagrams to support reasoning, and to help identify when sides or angles are equal. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may believe that the exterior and interior angles sum to 360°. • Students may believe that the sum of exterior angles depends on the number of sides the polygon has. • Students may assume that some equal angles always mean the polygon is regular, without checking that all sides are also equal in length. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 1 – Angles | Step 3 Mathematical talk • How can you identify the exterior angles of a polygon? • What is the relationship between an interior and an exterior angle in a polygon? • How can you determine if a polygon is regular? • What is the sum of the exterior angles of a polygon? • What is the formula to calculate the size of one exterior angle in a regular polygon? • What is the size of each exterior angle in a regular decagon? Explain how you know. • A regular polygon has exterior angles of 40°. How many sides does the polygon have? What is the mathematical name of the polygon? • An irregular pentagon has exterior angles of 82°, 102°, 37° and 90°. What is the size of the fifth exterior angle? H National Curriculum links • Derive and use the sum of angles in a triangle and use it to deduce the angle sum in any polygon and to derive properties of regular polygons (KS3)