Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Angles,4.0,4,1401,Interior angles of a polygon,Interior angles of a polygon,"In this small step, students recap their learning of interior angles of polygons from Key Stage 3. Begin by revisiting the relationship between interior and exterior angles. Highlight that they form a straight line and therefore sum to 180°. Remind students that the sum of interior angles can be calculated using (n – 2) × 180°, where n is the number of sides, and that this can also be used to work out the number of sides when the sum of interior angles is known. It can be useful to model splitting polygons into triangles to demonstrate why this method works to calculate the sum of interior angles. Reinforce that “regular” means that all sides and angles are equal. Calculators may be used throughout this step, so the focus remains on the method rather than the arithmetic. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may confuse interior and exterior angles. • Students may believe that the interior angle sum is the same for all polygons. • Students may believe the interior angle sum is found by multiplying the number of sides by 180°. • Students may believe that all interior angles are equal in size even when the polygon is irregular. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 1 – Angles | Step 4 Mathematical talk • What is the sum of adjacent interior and exterior angles? • A regular polygon has 12 sides. Work out the size of each interior angle. • A polygon has an interior angle sum of 900°. How many sides does the polygon have? • Can a polygon have an interior angle greater than 180°? Explain how you know. • Can two different polygons have the same interior angle sum? Explain how you know. • How can you calculate the interior angle sum of any polygon? • Can a polygon have a reflex interior angle? Explain how you know. H National Curriculum links • Derive and use the sum of angles in a triangle and use it to deduce the angle sum in any polygon, and to derive properties of regular polygons (KS3)","In this small step, students recap their learning of interior angles of polygons from Key Stage 3. Begin by revisiting the relationship between interior and exterior angles. Highlight that they form a straight line and therefore sum to 180°. Remind students that the sum of interior angles can be calculated using (n – 2) × 180°, where n is the number of sides, and that this can also be used to work out the number of sides when the sum of interior angles is known. It can be useful to model splitting polygons into triangles to demonstrate why this method works to calculate the sum of interior angles. Reinforce that “regular” means that all sides and angles are equal. Calculators may be used throughout this step, so the focus remains on the method rather than the arithmetic. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may confuse interior and exterior angles. • Students may believe that the interior angle sum is the same for all polygons. • Students may believe the interior angle sum is found by multiplying the number of sides by 180°. • Students may believe that all interior angles are equal in size even when the polygon is irregular. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 1 – Angles | Step 4 Mathematical talk • What is the sum of adjacent interior and exterior angles? • A regular polygon has 12 sides. Work out the size of each interior angle. • A polygon has an interior angle sum of 900°. How many sides does the polygon have? • Can a polygon have an interior angle greater than 180°? Explain how you know. • Can two different polygons have the same interior angle sum? Explain how you know. • How can you calculate the interior angle sum of any polygon? • Can a polygon have a reflex interior angle? Explain how you know. H National Curriculum links • Derive and use the sum of angles in a triangle and use it to deduce the angle sum in any polygon, and to derive properties of regular polygons (KS3)","In this small step, students recap their learning of interior a