Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Angles,8.0,8,1409,Prove geometric facts (E),Prove geometric facts (E),"In this extend step, students begin to use clear reasoning to prove geometric facts. Start by revisiting familiar angle facts, ensuring students can state each fact accurately. Model how to build a simple chain of reasoning using statements such as “because alternate angles are equal” or “because angles in a triangle sum to 180°”. Encourage students to write each step clearly and to justify their answers. Make sure that students understand that a proof must show a fact works in every case, not just for one example. Model to students how to structure their reasoning so it clearly shows a logical progression from the given information to the conclusion. Provide opportunities to spot errors in worked examples so they can recognise incomplete or incorrect reasoning. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may not justify their answers or give reasons for why they have applied certain angle facts. • Students may write conclusions without clearly showing the logical steps that lead to them. • Students may assume that diagrams are drawn to scale when they are not. • Students may believe they have proven a fact when they have only shown it works for one case. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 1 – Angles | Step 8 Mathematical talk • How can you tell which angle facts are needed to prove a statement? • Do angles in all triangles sum to 180°? Show how you know. • Is it possible for angles to be alternate if the lines are not parallel? Show how you know. • What do you notice about the sum of interior angles in a polygon when the number of sides increases by 1? • Does your reasoning work for all examples? How do you know? • Have you used correct mathematical language throughout your proof? Why is this important? • When two angles appear equal, how can you justify this rather than relying on the diagram? H National Curriculum links • Apply angle facts, triangle congruence, similarit