Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Graphs and diagrams,1.0,1,1412,Pie charts,Pie charts,"In this small step, students recap their learning from Year 8 on drawing and interpreting pie charts. Remind students that a pie chart represents a whole split into parts, with each sector showing a fraction of the total and all sectors summing to 360°. Make clear links between proportions, fractions and angles to show how they describe the same information. Emphasise the accurate use of a protractor when constructing sectors and highlight the importance of clear labelling. Introduce data given as percentages, fractions or bar charts for conversion into a pie chart. Encourage students to interpret pie charts by identifying key categories and, when comparing two charts, recognising that without totals only proportions can be compared. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may use the frequency as the angle size instead of working out the angle. • Students may not label the sectors. • When comparing two pie charts, students may assume equal sectors in different pie charts always represent the same frequency. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 2 – Graphs and diagrams | Step 1 Mathematical talk • What is the total frequency of the data set? • How can we calculate the angle for one category if we know its frequency and the total? • How does using the formula sector angle = frequency total × 360° calculate the number of degrees that is proportional to the total? • How can percentages be used to calculate sector angles? • How can you tell which category represents the greatest/least proportion of the whole? • If you know the angle of a sector on a pie chart, how can you work out what fraction of the whole this represents? • How could you work out how many students the sector represents if the total is ? H National Curriculum links • Construct and interpret appropriate tables, charts, and diagrams, including frequency tables, bar charts, pie charts, and pictograms for categorical data, and ve