Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Graphs and diagrams,2.0,2,1414,Time series graphs,Time series graphs,"In this small step, students recap their Year 7 learning on drawing and interpreting time series graphs. Remind them that these graphs show changes over time and require clearly labelled axes for accurate interpretation. Emphasise that time is always plotted on the horizontal axis and that equal intervals are essential. Encourage students to discuss upward or downward trends, along with periods of stability. Emphasise that while time series graphs highlight overall trends, short-term fluctuations may not always reflect the longer-term trend. Discuss with students how time series graphs with points joined by solid lines imply that it is possible to estimate information between data points, while points joined by dashed lines imply that the data between these points is not reliable. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may focus on specific points on a graph rather than the overall trends. • Students may assume that trends will always be consistent. • Students may use solid lines instead of dashed lines or vice versa. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 2 – Graphs and diagrams | Step 2 Mathematical talk • Does the time series graph have to start at zero? Explain your answer. • How can you show that your axis has not started from zero? • What does the overall trend of the graph show? • How can you describe the trend in this part of the graph? • Could there be a seasonal pattern? Explain your answer. • Is there a point that does not fit the general pattern? Why might that be? • Can the time series graph be used to estimate between data points? Explain your answer. • What similarities and what differences do you notice when comparing these two time series graphs? H National Curriculum links • Interpret and construct tables and line graphs for time series data","In this small step, students recap their Year 7 learning on drawing and interpreting time series graphs. Remind th