Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Graphs and diagrams,3.0,3,1416,Frequency polygons,Frequency polygons,"In this small step, students draw and interpret frequency polygons. Begin by modelling how to construct a frequency polygon by identifying the midpoint of each class interval, plotting these points and joining them with straight lines. Highlight that the horizontal axis shows data from the class intervals as a continuous scale and the vertical axis shows the frequencies. Discuss with students why midpoints of the class intervals are used to plot the data on the horizontal axis and to describe what the shape of the frequency polygon shows, such as increases or decreases in the data. Model how to compare two frequency polygons on the same axes and explain how the overall shape helps students interpret and compare different distributions. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may plot the class boundaries rather than the midpoint. • Students may join the first and last points to form a polygon. • Students may draw bars instead of a polygon. • Students may incorrectly join the points using a curve instead of straight lines. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 2 – Graphs and diagrams | Step 3 Mathematical talk • What is the difference between a midpoint and an endpoint? • Given the boundaries of a class interval, how do you work out the midpoint? • Why do we plot the midpoint instead of the class boundaries? • How does the scale on a set of axes affect how a frequency polygon looks? • What does the steepest part of the polygon tell you about the data? • Does the line between two points represent actual data or an estimate? Explain how you know. H National Curriculum links • Interpret, analyse and compare the distributions of data sets from univariate empirical distributions through: appropriate graphical representation involving discrete, continuous and grouped data, {including box plots}","In this small step, students draw and interpret frequency polygons. Begin by modellin