Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Graphs and diagrams,4.0,4,1418,Stem-and-leaf diagrams,Stem-and-leaf diagrams,"In this small step, students draw and interpret stem-and-leaf diagrams. Begin by explaining that these diagrams organise numerical data, using the stem to show place value and the leaf to show the final digit, allowing all values to remain visible. Emphasise that including a clear key is essential so the data can be correctly interpreted. Model how to organise the data, select a suitable stem, and record each leaf clearly so digits align. Revisit how to calculate averages and the range by finding the difference between the greatest and smallest values in the set. Include examples with decimal values, such as 7|3 representing 7.3 As students develop confidence, introduce back-to-back stem- and-leaf diagrams to compare two data sets. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may write leaves out of order instead of listing them from smallest to largest. • Students may forget to include a key, which can make it difficult to interpret the values represented. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 2 – Graphs and diagrams | Step 4 Mathematical talk • What does the stem represent in this diagram? How do you know? • Why do we list the leaves in ascending order rather than the order they were collected? • Why is a key important when interpreting the values from a stem-and-leaf diagram? • What is the smallest value and what is the largest? How do you know? • What does the length of each row suggest about the distribution of values? • How would this diagram change if the data included decimals? • When might a back-to-back stem-and-leaf diagram be useful? H National Curriculum links • Interpret, analyse and compare the distributions of data sets from univariate empirical distributions through: appropriate graphical representation involving discrete, continuous and grouped data, {including box plots}","In this small step, students draw and interpret stem-and-leaf diagrams. Begin by