Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Graphs and diagrams,6.0,6,1422,Interpret histograms,Interpret histograms,"In this small step, students build on their understanding of drawing histograms to now interpret them. Recap that histograms show continuous data and that the area of each bar represents frequency. Guide students to examine a single bar and identify what they know, such as the class boundaries and the height. Ask students how these two features could be used to calculate the frequency. Support students to derive the relationship, frequency = frequency density × class width. Provide opportunities to estimate values within an interval, including estimating a value for the median by accumulating frequencies. Encourage students to express parts of the distribution as fractions and percentages once they have calculated the total frequency. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may believe the frequency is represented by the height of the bar rather than the area. • Students may use class midpoints rather than class boundaries when estimating values. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 2 – Graphs and diagrams | Step 6 Mathematical talk • What does the area of each bar represent? • Why is the height alone not enough to work out the frequency? • If you know the class width and the frequency density, how can you work out the frequencies? • How can you work out the frequency of each class from the histogram? • How can you work out the total frequency represented by the diagram? • How can you identify the class that contains the median? • If we know the total frequency, how can you find the fraction or percentage of the data that lies in a certain interval? • How can you estimate the median on a histogram? H National Curriculum links • {construct and interpret diagrams for grouped discrete data and continuous data, i.e. histograms with equal and unequal class intervals and cumulative frequency graphs, and know their appropriate use}","In this small step, students build on thei