Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Graphs and diagrams,7.0,7,1424,Draw cumulative frequency diagrams,Draw cumulative frequency diagrams,"In this small step, students explore how cumulative frequency diagrams are constructed from grouped data. Begin by discussing how the total builds as we add on the frequency from each class interval and guide students to recognise that cumulative frequency represents a running total. Highlight that these totals are plotted at the upper boundary of the class interval. Ask students to discuss the shape of the diagram, noticing that it can rise or stay level but never fall. Highlight that a cumulative frequency diagram can be plotted with either a smooth curve or straight line segments. It can be helpful to demonstrate the plotting process under a visualiser so students can watch each step clearly as the cumulative frequency diagram is formed. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may plot cumulative frequencies at the midpoint of class boundaries instead of the upper boundaries. • Students may plot frequencies from a given table rather than a cumulative frequency. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 2 – Graphs and diagrams | Step 7 Mathematical talk • What does the cumulative frequency tell us about the data up to a given point? • How does adding each class frequency change the cumulative total? • Are cumulative frequency diagrams always plotted at the upper boundary? Explain your answer. • Can the shape of a cumulative frequency diagram show a decrease? Why or why not? • How are the plotted points joined? • What is the same and what is different between a frequency polygon and a cumulative frequency diagram? H National Curriculum links • {construct and interpret diagrams for grouped discrete data and continuous data, i.e. histograms with equal and unequal class intervals and cumulative frequency graphs and know their appropriate use}","In this small step, students explore how cumulative frequency diagrams are constructed from grouped data.