Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Graphs and diagrams,8.0,8,1426,Interpret cumulative frequency diagrams,Interpret cumulative frequency diagrams,"In this small step, students develop their understanding of cumulative frequency diagrams by interpreting the data shown. Remind students that each plotted point represents a cumulative total. Guide students to identify an estimate for the median and lower and upper quartiles by finding 25%, 50% and 75% of the total frequency and locating these values on the curve. Students then calculate the interquartile range by subtracting the lower quartile from the upper quartile. They should also be able to compare two cumulative frequency diagrams by considering the median and interquartile range and deduce what these comparisons indicate. Encourage accurate reading of values by drawing horizontal and vertical lines and discuss why this method is more reliable than estimating by eye. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may attempt to find quartiles using the horizontal axis instead of the cumulative frequency axis. • Students may read values directly from the curve without drawing horizontal and vertical lines, resulting in inaccuracies. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 2 – Graphs and diagrams | Step 8 Mathematical talk • What does each plotted point represent on a cumulative frequency diagram? • How do we know where to find 25%, 50% and 75% of the data? • Why are the values for the median, upper and lower quartiles only an estimate? • What does the interquartile range tell us about the distribution of the data? • What does the graph tell us about the value below which 10% of the data lies? • Why can’t the range be identified from a grouped frequency table or a cumulative frequency table? H National Curriculum links • {construct and interpret diagrams for grouped discrete data and continuous data, i.e. histograms with equal and unequal class intervals and cumulative frequency graphs and know their appropriate use} • interpret, analy