Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Graphs and diagrams,10.0,10,1428,Compare distributions using box plots (E),Compare distributions using box plots (E),"In this small step, students learn how to compare distributions using box plots. Remind students that a box plot summarises a data set using the minimum, lower quartile, median, upper quartile and maximum values, allowing two or more distributions to be compared quickly on the same scale. Emphasise that comparisons should focus on a measure of average (median) and spread (range or interquartile range). Highlight that the relative positions of the medians can be used to decide which data set has the higher typical value. Encourage students to compare lengths of the boxes to judge differences in the interquartile ranges and discuss which data set shows greater consistency. Highlight the importance of using the same continuous scale so that comparisons are valid. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may draw incorrect conclusions, for example interpreting a larger range as an average or vice versa. • Students may incorrectly interpret the interquartile range or range, for example thinking a larger interquartile range is ‘better’. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 2 – Graphs and diagrams | Step 10 Mathematical talk • How do the positions of the medians help you compare trends in data sets? • Why is analysing the medians alone not enough to compare two data sets? • Which distribution shows a greater spread of data? How do you know? • How do the interquartile ranges compare? • What does a smaller interquartile range reveal about the spread of the middle half of the data? • How can you tell which data set is more consistent? • What do the “whiskers” tell you about the extremes in each data set? • What is the difference between the range and the interquartile range? Which of these measures might be affected by an outlier? H National Curriculum links • Interpret, analyse and compare the distributions of data sets from univariate