Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Vectors,2.0,2,1432,Vector notation,Vector notation,"In this small step, students build on their understanding of vector representations as column vectors and directed line segments by introducing notation for labelling vectors. Different conventions should be introduced, such as writing a vector as AB , emphasising that this represents the vector from A to B. Comparing related vectors can further deepen understanding of vectors as movements between points, for example by comparing AB with BA . Students should also be given opportunities to work with vectors labelled using bold letters, such as a or x. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may misinterpret vectors, for example reading XY as YX . • Students may neglect to draw arrows on the vectors or draw arrows in the wrong direction. • Students may neglect to label vectors. For example, not labelling ‘E’ and ‘F’ on either end of a line segment to represent the vector EF . Year 10 | Summer term | Block 3 – Vectors | Step 2 Mathematical talk • What is the significance of the order of the letters in the vector AB ? How does this differ from the vector BA ? • Do the letters describing a vector always have to be in alphabetical order? Explain how you know. • If vector a = 3 4   what would –a represent? Explain how you know. • How can two vectors have the same magnitude but different directions? Write an example. • If CD = 2 3  , how would you represent CD on a grid? • If CD = 2 3  , how would you represent DC on a grid? H National Curriculum links • Describe translations as 2D vectors • Apply addition and subtraction of vectors, multiplication of vectors by a scalar, and diagrammatic and column representations of vectors; {use vectors to construct geometric arguments and proofs}","In this small step, students build on their understanding of vector representations as column vectors and directed line segments by introducing notation for labelling vectors. Different conventions shoul