Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Vectors,3.0,3,1434,Vectors multiplied by a scalar,Vectors multiplied by a scalar,"In this small step, students explore vectors that are parallel to each other. Introduce students to the concept of a scalar, emphasising that it is the value by which a vector is multiplied. Students should recognise that when one vector is a scalar multiple of another, the two vectors are parallel. Begin by using diagrams to represent different vectors, for example by comparing a and 2a on a grid, highlighting that both are parallel and that 2a is twice the magnitude of a. This can then be used to build towards multiplying column vectors by a scalar. Emphasise that both the horizontal and vertical components of a column vector are multiplied by the scalar. Students should also have opportunities to explore the effect of multiplying vectors by negative scalars; for example, multiplying by –1 reverses the direction of a vector. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may make arithmetic errors, particularly when calculating with negative numbers. • Students may neglect to multiply both values in a column vector by the scalar. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 3 – Vectors | Step 3 Mathematical talk • What is the same and what is different about parallel vectors? • Can two vectors be parallel but not equal in magnitude? Explain how you know. • How do we know if one vector is a multiple of the other? • What is a scalar? • What is the effect of multiplying a vector by a negative scalar? • How does multiplying a vector by a scalar change its magnitude? Does it ever change its direction? H National Curriculum links • Describe translations as 2D vectors • Apply addition and subtraction of vectors, multiplication of vectors by a scalar, and diagrammatic and column representations of vectors; {use vectors to construct geometric arguments and proofs}","In this small step, students explore vectors that are parallel to each other. Introduce students to the concept of a s