Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Vectors,8.0,8,1443,Parallel vectors,Parallel vectors,"In this small step, students explore parallel vectors, recognising that vectors are parallel when one is a scalar multiple of the other. Students may have some understanding of this concept from earlier steps in this block. Highlight that parallel vectors have the same (or opposite) direction but may differ in magnitude. Support strategies for proving that column vectors are parallel by using vectors represented on grids to emphasise the link between vectors and scalars. After this, model how to prove that algebraic vectors are parallel through factorisation. Once students are confident, move on to more complex problems where they may need to consider vector journeys to compare two vectors. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may think vectors are parallel if they have the same magnitude. • Students may not recognise parallel vectors involving negative scalars. For example, writing 6 2 -   as –2 × -3 1   to show 6 2 -   and -3 1   are parallel. • Students may find it difficult to write algebraic expressions for vectors that represent movement in the opposite direction. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 3 – Vectors | Step 8 Mathematical talk • What does it mean for two vectors to be parallel? • How can we tell if one column vector is a multiple of another? • Can two vectors be parallel but point in opposite directions? Explain how you know. • How can we tell if two vectors in terms of a and b are parallel? • What happens to a vector when it is multiplied by a negative scalar? • Why do parallel vectors not have to be equal in length? • Are two vectors that are equal in magnitude always parallel? Why or why not? H National Curriculum links • Describe translations as 2D vectors • Apply addition and subtraction of vectors, multiplication of vectors by a scalar, and diagrammatic and column representations of vectors; {use vectors to construct geometric arguments and proofs}",