Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Factors and powers,1.0,1,1445,"Prime factorisation, HCF and LCM","Prime factorisation, HCF and LCM","In this small step, students recap finding the highest common factor (HCF), lowest common multiple (LCM), and writing numbers as a product of prime factors from Key Stage 3. Use practical contexts to reinforce when HCF or LCM is needed, and model listing with small numbers to show why it becomes inefficient for greater values. Revisit prime factorisation using factor trees, emphasising that every integer greater than 1 has a unique prime factorisation. Use Venn diagrams to organise information, highlighting that the HCF is the product of the common factors, while the LCM is the product of all factors within the diagram. Link back to listing to show both methods give the same result. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may include non-prime factors in their final product. • Students may confuse factors and multiples, particularly when using listing methods. • Students may rely on listing methods for large numbers, leading to inefficiency and increased likelihood of error. • Students may multiply all prime factors when finding the HCF, rather than only those common to all numbers. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 4 – Factors, powers and surds | Step 1 Mathematical talk • Explain what is meant by “highest common factor”. • Explain what is meant by “lowest common multiple”. • How can we decide whether a problem requires us to calculate the HCF or the LCM? • Can the HCF ever be greater than one of the numbers? Why or why not? • Is there more than one way to express 84 as a product of prime factors? • Does the order of the factors in a prime factorisation matter? Explain your answer. • What is the same and what is different between 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 and 23 × 3? • How do you find the HCF using a Venn diagram? • How do you find the LCM using a Venn diagram? H National Curriculum links • Use the concepts and vocabulary of prime numbers, factors (o