Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Factors and powers,3.0,3,1449,Fractional indices,Fractional indices,"In this small step, students explore fractional indices. Some students may have previously covered this as an extend step in Year 8. Ensure students are fluent with square roots, cube roots and higher roots before introducing fractional indices using numerical bases. Develop understanding by reasoning from index laws, for example 9 1 2 × 9 1 2 = 91, and link this to 9 = 3 since 3 × 3 = 9. Use this to generalise that the denominator of a fractional index indicates the root. Once students are confident with unit fractional indices, introduce non-unit fractional indices, emphasising that the numerator represents a power applied after the root. Extend to fractional bases and negative fractional indices by linking back to negative indices as reciprocals. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may confuse the roles of the numerator and denominator, for example interpreting 64 3 2 as a cube root squared instead of a square root cubed. • Students may attempt to calculate a fraction of the base rather than taking the root and then applying the power. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 4 – Factors, powers and surds | Step 3 Mathematical talk • Explain why a 1 2 × a 1 2 = a1 • If 4 1 2 × 4 1 2 = 41, what is the value of 4 1 2 ? • What operation is a fractional power equivalent to? • What root is a power of a half/third equivalent to? Explain how you know. • How else can x 1 4 be written? • How can you generalise a rule for fractional indices? • If you know the value of x 1 3 , how can you find the value of – x 1 3? • What is the same and what is different about calculating 2 –3 64 and 3 –2 64 ? H National Curriculum links • Calculate with roots, and with integer {and fractional} indices","In this small step, students explore fractional indices. Some students may have previously covered this as an extend step in Year 8. Ensure students are fluent with square roots, cube roots and hi