Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Factors and powers,4.0,4,1451,Four operations with surds,Four operations with surds,"In this small step, students work with all four operations involving surds. The focus is on adding/subtracting like surds and multiplying/dividing surds by surds/integers. At this stage, surds do not need to be simplified beyond an integer result. For example, 3 × 5 can remain as 15, while 4 × 4 simplifies to 4. This keeps the focus on structure rather than technique, with further surd simplification developed in the next step. When adding and subtracting, emphasise that only like surds can be combined, just as only like algebraic terms can be collected, for example, 3 2 + 5 2 = 8 2 but 2 + 3 cannot be simplified further. When multiplying, model that a × b æ ab and that multiplying by an integer scales the surd, for example a × b = a b. If students are secure, you may choose to combine this step with simplifying surds further. Misconceptions and common errors • Students may try to add or subtract surds with different bases, for example 2 + 3 • Students may multiply integers and surds incorrectly, for example writing 2 × 5 = 10 • Students may not recognise when the product of surds simplifies to an integer, such as 4 × 4 = 4 Year 10 | Summer term | Block 4 – Factors, powers and surds | Step 4 Mathematical talk • Are surds rational or irrational? • When we multiply/divide two surds, what happens to the numbers inside the square roots? • Explain why 4 × 4 is equivalent to 4 • Is a + a equivalent to 2a ? Why or why not? • Why does 2 × 3 not give an integer answer? • What stays the same and what changes when we multiply a surd by an integer? • Why can we not add surds with different bases, for example 2 + 3? • How is adding and subtracting surds similar to working with algebraic terms? H National Curriculum links • Simplify and manipulate algebraic expressions (including those involving surds) {and algebraic fractions} by: factorising quadratic expressions of 