Year 10,14-15,Summer,Higher,Factors and powers,8.0,8,1459,Expand double brackets with surds,Expand double brackets with surds,"In this small step, students extend their understanding of expanding single brackets with surds to expanding and simplifying double brackets. Model expansion by multiplying each term in the first bracket by each term in the second bracket, making all four products explicit. Area or grid models can help students see the structure and avoid missing terms. When simplifying after expansion, remind students that only like surds can be combined, and that surds may need further simplification by taking out square factors. This may lead to integer results or simpler surd forms. Once students are confident, they can be challenged with more complex expressions in the form (a + b c)(d + e c). Misconceptions and common errors • Students may only multiply the first and last terms in each brackets, for example writing ( 2 + 3)( 2 + 5) = 2 + 15 = 17 • Students may incorrectly simplify expressions, for example 10 + 5 2 + 2 + 2 = 19 • Students may not simplify products of surds where possible after expansion or may not realise it is possible for the final answer to be an integer. Year 10 | Summer term | Block 4 – Factors, powers and surds | Step 8 Mathematical talk • What is the same and what is different about expanding (x + 2)(x + 5) and ( x + 2)( x + 5)? • When we multiply two surds, what happens to the numbers inside the square root? Explain your answer. • Explain why 3 × 3 = 9 can be simplified, but 2 × 5 = 10 cannot be simplified further. • What do you notice when (a + b)(a – b) is expanded and simplified? Why do the surd terms cancel when simplifying? • What is the difference between simplifying a × a and simplifying a + a? • Can any of the surds in the resulting expansion be simplified further? Why or why not? H National Curriculum links • Simplify and manipulate algebraic expressions (including those involving surds) {and algebraic fractions} by: fa