Year 10,14-15,Summer,Foundation,Factors and powers,9.0,9,1460,Expand brackets with surds (E),Expand brackets with surds (E),"In this extend step, students develop their understanding of surds by expanding and simplifying single brackets involving surds. Begin with expanding single brackets and remind students that surds behave like algebraic terms and that the distributive law applies. Model expansion by multiplying each term inside the bracket by the term outside, using bar models or area models to support understanding. Once confident, students could be introduced to expanding and simplifying double brackets. Emphasise that only like surds can be combined. At this stage, avoid simplifying surds by factorising square factors, and instead focus on products that simplify directly to integers, such as 4 × 4 = 4 Misconceptions and common errors • Students may try to combine unlike surds after expansion, for example trying to simplify 2 + 3 • Students may assume a × a = a rather than a. • Students may multiply the integer and the surd incorrectly, for example writing 2 × 5 = 10 Year 10 | Summer term | Block 4 – Factors, powers and surds | Step 9 Mathematical talk • What is the same and what is different about expanding 3(x + 5) and 3( 2 + 5)? • What is the same and what is different about multiplying 3 × a and 3 × 5? • When we multiply two surds, what happens to the values inside the square root? • Explain why 2 × 2 = 4 can be simplified to an integer but 2 × 5 = 10 cannot. • Explain why 2 3 + 5 2 cannot be simplified. • What makes two surds “like surds”? Give an example. • Explain how you know when an expression with surds is fully simplified. F National Curriculum links • Simplify and manipulate algebraic expressions (including those involving surds) by: factorising quadratic expressions of the form x2 + bx + c, including the difference of two squares; simplifying expressions involving sums, products and powers, including the laws of indices E","In this extend step, s